Brusselian, Brusseler

[LINK: (fr) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_language]  Bruxellois(e)

[LINK: (nl) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch_language]  Brusselaar/Brusselse

Brussels, [LINK: ([a]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-fn1-12]  officially the Brussels-Capital Region, [LINK: ([b]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-fn2-13]  [LINK: ([12]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-Belgian_Constitution-14]  [LINK: ([13]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-CIRB-creation-15]  is a  [LINK: (region) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Communities,_regions_and_language_areas_of_Belgium#Regions]  of  [LINK: (Belgium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgium]  comprising  [LINK: (19 municipalities) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#Municipalities] , including the  [LINK: (City of Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_of_Brussels] , which is the capital of Belgium. [LINK: ([14]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-article_194-16]  The Brussels-Capital Region is located in the central portion of the country. It is a part of both the  [LINK: (French Community of Belgium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_Community_of_Belgium]  [LINK: ([15]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-décret-17]  and the  [LINK: (Flemish Community) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flemish_Community] , [LINK: ([16]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-18]  and is separate from the  [LINK: (Flemish Region) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flemish_Region]  (Flanders), within which it forms an  [LINK: (enclave) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enclave] , and the  [LINK: (Walloon Region) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walloon_Region]  (Wallonia), located less than 4 kilometres (2.5 mi) to the south. [LINK: ([17]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-decreet-19]  [LINK: ([18]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-20]

Brussels grew from a small rural settlement on the river  [LINK: (Senne) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Senne_(river)]  to become an important city-region in Europe. Since the end of the  [LINK: (Second World War) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_World_War] , it has been a major centre for  [LINK: (international politics) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_politics]  and home to numerous international organisations, politicians,  [LINK: (diplomats) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diplomacy]  and  [LINK: (civil servants) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Civil_servant] . [LINK: ([19]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-BBC_Belgium-21]  Brussels is the  [LINK: (de facto) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_facto]  capital of the  [LINK: (European Union) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Union] , as it hosts a number of principal  [LINK: (EU institutions) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/EU_institutions] , including its  [LINK: (administrative-legislative) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Commission] ,  [LINK: (executive-political) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Council] , and  [LINK: (legislative) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Parliament]  branches (though the judicial branch is located in  [LINK: (Luxembourg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luxembourg_City] , and the European Parliament meets for a minority of the year in  [LINK: (Strasbourg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strasbourg] ). [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEDemey2007-1]  [LINK: ([20]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-12010E/PRO/06-22]  [LINK: ([c]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-23]  Because of this, its name is sometimes used  [LINK: (metonymically) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metonymy]  to describe the EU and its institutions. [LINK: ([21]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-24]  [LINK: ([22]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-25]  The secretariat of the  [LINK: (Benelux) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benelux]  and the  [LINK: (headquarters) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NATO_Headquarters]  of  [LINK: (NATO) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NATO]  are also located in Brussels. [LINK: ([23]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-26]  [LINK: ([24]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-27]

Brussels is the most densely populated region in Belgium, and although it has the highest  [LINK: (GDP per capita) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GDP_per_capita] , [LINK: ([25]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-:4-28]  it has the lowest available income per household. [LINK: ([26]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-statbel-29]  The Brussels Region covers 162 km2 (63 sq mi) and has a population of over 1.2 million. [LINK: ([27]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-:8-30]  Its five times larger  [LINK: (metropolitan area) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_metropolitan_area]  comprises over 2.5 million people, which makes it the  [LINK: (largest in Belgium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metropolitan_areas_in_Belgium] . [LINK: ([28]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-BelMun2008-31]  [LINK: ([29]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-BelMetr-32]  [LINK: ([30]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-:5-33]  It is also part of a large  [LINK: (conurbation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conurbation]  extending towards the cities of  [LINK: (Ghent) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghent] ,  [LINK: (Antwerp) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp] , and  [LINK: (Leuven) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leuven] , known as the  [LINK: (Flemish Diamond) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flemish_Diamond] , as well as the province of  [LINK: (Walloon Brabant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walloon_Brabant] , in total home to over 5 million people. [LINK: ([31]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEVan_MeeterenBoussauwDerudderWitlox2016-34]  As Belgium's economic capital and a top financial centre in Western Europe with  [LINK: (Euronext Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euronext_Brussels] , Brussels is classified as an Alpha  [LINK: (global city) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_city] . [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-35]  It is also a national and international hub for rail, road and air traffic, [LINK: ([33]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-36]  and is sometimes considered, together with Belgium, as Europe's geographic, economic and cultural crossroads. [LINK: ([34]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-37]  [LINK: ([35]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-38]  [LINK: ([36]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-39]  The  [LINK: (Brussels Metro) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Metro]  is the only  [LINK: (rapid transit) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rapid_transit]  system in Belgium. In addition, both its  [LINK: (airport) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Airport]  and  [LINK: (railway stations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_railway_stations_in_Belgium]  are the largest and busiest in the country. [LINK: ([37]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-:2-40]  [LINK: ([38]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-41]

Historically Dutch-speaking, Brussels saw a  [LINK: (language shift to French) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francization_of_Brussels]  from the late 19th century. [LINK: ([39]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-42]  Since its creation in 1989, the Brussels-Capital Region has been officially bilingual in French and Dutch, [LINK: ([40]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-43]  [LINK: ([41]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-44]  although French is the majority language and  [LINK: (lingua franca) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lingua_franca] . [LINK: ([42]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-BruS13EN-45]  Brussels is also increasingly becoming multilingual. English is spoken widely and many migrants and  [LINK: (expatriates) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Expatriate]  speak other languages as well. [LINK: ([42]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-BruS13EN-45]  [LINK: ([43]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-:7-46]

Brussels is known for its cuisine and gastronomic offer (including its local  [LINK: (waffle) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_waffle] , its  [LINK: (chocolate) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_chocolate] , its  [LINK: (French fries) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_fries]  and its numerous types of  [LINK: (beers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beer_in_Belgium] ), [LINK: ([44]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-47]  as well as its historical and architectural landmarks; some of them are registered as  [LINK: (UNESCO) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UNESCO]   [LINK: (World Heritage Sites) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Heritage_Site] . [LINK: ([45]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-48]  Principal attractions include its historic  [LINK: (Grand-Place/Grote Markt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand-Place]  (main square),  [LINK: (Manneken Pis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manneken_Pis] , the  [LINK: (Atomium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atomium] , and cultural institutions such as  [LINK: (La Monnaie/De Munt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Monnaie/De_Munt]  and the  [LINK: (Museums of Art and History) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Museums_of_Art_and_History] . Due to its long tradition of  [LINK: (Belgian comics) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_comics] , Brussels is also hailed as a capital of the  [LINK: (comic strip) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comic_strip] . [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-Comic-3]  [LINK: ([46]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-:1-49]


# TOPONYMY #

The most common theory of the origin of the name Brussels is that it derives from the  [LINK: (Old Dutch) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Dutch]  Bruocsella, Broekzele or Broeksel, meaning 'marsh' (bruoc / broek) and 'home, settlement' (sella / zele / sel) or 'settlement in the marsh'. [LINK: ([47]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-50]  [LINK: ([48]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-vgc-51]  Saint  [LINK: (Vindicianus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vindicianus] , the Bishop of  [LINK: (Cambrai) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cambrai] , made the first recorded reference to the place Brosella in 695, [LINK: ([49]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-52]  when it was still a  [LINK: (hamlet) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamlet_(place)] . The names of all the municipalities in the Brussels-Capital Region are also of Dutch origin, except for  [LINK: (Evere) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evere] , which is possibly  [LINK: (Celtic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Celtic_languages]  or  [LINK: (Old Frankish) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Frankish] . [LINK: ([50]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTETribotDefawe20063-53]

In French, Bruxelles is pronounced  [LINK: ([bʁysɛl]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/French]   [LINK: (ⓘ) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Fr-Bruxelles.ogg]  (the x is pronounced  [LINK: (/s/) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/English]  and the final s is silent) and in Dutch, Brussel is pronounced  [LINK: ([ˈbrʏsəl]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/Dutch]   [LINK: (ⓘ) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Nl-Brussel.ogg] . Inhabitants of Brussels are known in French as Bruxellois (pronounced  [LINK: ([bʁysɛlwa]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/French]   [LINK: (ⓘ) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Fr-bruxellois.ogg] ) and in Dutch as Brusselaars (pronounced  [LINK: ([ˈbrʏsəlaːrs]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/Dutch] ). In the  [LINK: (Brabantian dialect) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brabantian_dialect]  of Brussels (known as  [LINK: (Brusselian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brusselian_dialect] , and also sometimes referred to as Marols or Marollien), [LINK: ([51]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTETreffers-Daller199425-54]  they are called Brusseleers or Brusseleirs. [LINK: ([52]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-evans-55]

Originally, the written x was pronounced  [LINK: (/ks/) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/English] . In the  [LINK: (Belgian French) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_French]  pronunciation as well as in Dutch, the k eventually disappeared, as reflected in the current  [LINK: (Dutch spelling) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch_spelling] , whereas in the more conservative  [LINK: (French form) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_orthography] , the spelling remained. [LINK: ([53]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-56]  The pronunciation  [LINK: (/ks/) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/English]  in French only dates from the 18th century, but this modification did not affect the traditional Brussels usage. In France, the pronunciations  [LINK: ([bʁyksɛl]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/French]   [LINK: (ⓘ) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Fr-Bruxelles.oga]  and  [LINK: ([bʁyksɛlwa]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/French]  (for bruxellois) are often heard, but are rather rare in Belgium. [LINK: ([54]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-Lerond,_1980_pp._477-57]


# HISTORY #

The history of Brussels is closely linked to that of  [LINK: (Western Europe) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_Europe] . Traces of human settlement go back to the  [LINK: (Stone Age) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stone_Age] , with vestiges and place-names related to the civilisation of  [LINK: (megaliths) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Megalith] ,  [LINK: (dolmens) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dolmen]  and  [LINK: (standing stones) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Standing_stones]  (Plattesteen near the  [LINK: (Grand-Place/Grote Markt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand-Place]  and  [LINK: (Tomberg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomberg_metro_station]  in  [LINK: (Woluwe-Saint-Lambert) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woluwe-Saint-Lambert] , for example). During  [LINK: (late antiquity) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Late_antiquity] , the region was home to  [LINK: (Roman) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Empire]  occupation, as attested by archaeological evidence discovered on the current site of  [LINK: (Tour & Taxis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tour_%26_Taxis] , north-west of the  [LINK: (Pentagon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pentagon_(Brussels)]  (Brussels' city centre). [LINK: ([55]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-58]  [LINK: ([56]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-59]  Following the decline of the  [LINK: (Western Roman Empire) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_Roman_Empire] , it was incorporated into the  [LINK: (Frankish Empire) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frankish_Empire] .

According to local legend, the origin of the settlement that was to become Brussels lies in Saint  [LINK: (Gaugericus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaugericus] ' construction of a chapel on  [LINK: (an island) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint-G%C3%A9ry_Island]  in the river  [LINK: (Senne) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Senne_(river)]  around 580. [LINK: ([57]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEState2004269-60]  The official founding of Brussels is usually said to be around 979, when  [LINK: (Charles, Duke of Lower Lorraine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles,_Duke_of_Lower_Lorraine] , transferred the  [LINK: (relics) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Relic]  of the  [LINK: (martyr) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martyr]  Saint  [LINK: (Gudula) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gudula]  from  [LINK: (Moorsel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moorsel]  (located in today's province of  [LINK: (East Flanders) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_Flanders] ) to Saint Gaugericus' chapel. When  [LINK: (Otto II, Holy Roman Emperor) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Otto_II,_Holy_Roman_Emperor] , appointed the same Charles to become Duke of  [LINK: (Lower Lotharingia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lower_Lotharingia]  in 977, [LINK: ([58]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTERiché1983276-61]  Charles ordered the construction of the city's first permanent fortification, doing so on that same island.

[LINK: (Lambert I, Count of Louvain) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lambert_I,_Count_of_Louvain] , gained the County of Brussels around 1000 by marrying Charles' daughter. Because of its location on the banks of the Senne, on an  [LINK: (important trade route) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brabant_Road]  between the  [LINK: (Flemish) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/County_of_Flanders]  cities of  [LINK: (Bruges) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bruges]  and  [LINK: (Ghent) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghent] , and  [LINK: (Cologne) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cologne]  in the  [LINK: (Kingdom of Germany) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Germany] , Brussels became a commercial centre specialised in the textile trade. The town grew quite rapidly and extended towards the upper town (Treurenberg,  [LINK: (Coudenberg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coudenberg]  and  [LINK: (Sablon/Zavel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sablon,_Brussels]  areas), where there was a reduced risk of floods. As the town grew to a population of around 30,000, the surrounding marshes were drained to allow for further expansion. In 1183, the Counts of Leuven became  [LINK: (Dukes of Brabant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dukes_of_Brabant] . Brabant, unlike the county of Flanders, was not fief of the king of France but was incorporated into the  [LINK: (Holy Roman Empire) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holy_Roman_Empire] .

In the early 13th century, the  [LINK: (first walls of Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_walls_of_Brussels]  were built [LINK: ([59]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-62]  and after this, the city grew significantly. Around this time, work began on what is now the  [LINK: (Cathedral of St. Michael and St. Gudula) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cathedral_of_St._Michael_and_St._Gudula]  (1225), replacing an older  [LINK: (Romanesque) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romanesque_architecture]  church. [LINK: ([60]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBrigode1938185–215-63]  To let the city expand,  [LINK: (a second set of walls) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_walls_of_Brussels]  was erected between 1356 and 1383. Traces of these walls can still be seen; the  [LINK: (Small Ring) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Small_Ring,_Brussels] , a series of boulevards bounding the historical city centre, follows their former course.

In the 14th century, the marriage between heiress  [LINK: (Margaret III, Countess of Flanders) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_III,_Countess_of_Flanders] , and  [LINK: (Philip the Bold) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_the_Bold] ,  [LINK: (Duke of Burgundy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duke_of_Burgundy] , produced a new Duke of Brabant of the  [LINK: (House of Valois) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Valois] , namely  [LINK: (Anthony) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthony,_Duke_of_Brabant] , their son. [LINK: ([61]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBlockmansPrevenier199930–31-64]  In 1477, the Burgundian duke  [LINK: (Charles the Bold) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_the_Bold]  perished in the  [LINK: (Battle of Nancy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Nancy] . [LINK: ([62]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKirk1868542-65]  Through the marriage of his daughter  [LINK: (Mary of Burgundy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_of_Burgundy]  (who was born in Brussels) to Holy Roman Emperor  [LINK: (Maximilian I) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maximilian_I,_Holy_Roman_Emperor] , the  [LINK: (Low Countries) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Low_Countries]  fell under  [LINK: (Habsburg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Habsburg]  sovereignty. [LINK: ([63]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEArmstrong1957228-66]  Brabant was integrated into this composite state, and Brussels flourished as the Princely Capital of the prosperous  [LINK: (Burgundian Netherlands) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burgundian_Netherlands] , also known as the  [LINK: (Seventeen Provinces) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seventeen_Provinces] . After the death of Mary in 1482, her son  [LINK: (Philip the Handsome) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_the_Handsome]  succeeded as Duke of Burgundy and Brabant.

Philip died in 1506, and he was succeeded by his son  [LINK: (Charles V) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_V,_Holy_Roman_Emperor]  who then also became  [LINK: (King of Spain) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_of_Spain]  (crowned in the  [LINK: (Cathedral of St. Michael and St. Gudula) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cathedral_of_St._Michael_and_St._Gudula] ) and even Holy Roman Emperor at the death of his grandfather Maximilian I in 1519. Charles was now the ruler of a  [LINK: (Habsburg Empire) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Habsburg_Empire]  "on which the sun never sets" with Brussels serving as one of his main capitals. [LINK: ([2]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-Capital-2]  [LINK: ([64]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-67]  It was in the  [LINK: (Coudenberg Palace) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coudenberg_Palace]  that Charles V was declared of age in 1515, and it was there in 1555 that he abdicated all of his possessions and passed the  [LINK: (Habsburg Netherlands) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Habsburg_Netherlands]  to King  [LINK: (Philip II of Spain) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_II_of_Spain] . [LINK: ([65]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWasseige19954-68]  This palace, famous all over Europe, had greatly expanded since it had first become the seat of the Dukes of Brabant, but it was destroyed by fire in 1731. [LINK: ([66]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMardaga1994222-69]  [LINK: ([67]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWasseige19956–7-70]

In the 16th and 17th centuries, Brussels was a centre for the  [LINK: (lace) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lace]  industry. In addition,  [LINK: (Brussels tapestry) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_tapestry]  hung on the walls of castles throughout Europe. [LINK: ([68]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-71]  [LINK: ([69]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-72]  In 1695, during the  [LINK: (Nine Years' War) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nine_Years%27_War] , King  [LINK: (Louis XIV) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_XIV]  of France sent troops to  [LINK: (bombard Brussels with artillery) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bombardment_of_Brussels_(1695)] . Together with the resulting fire, it was the most destructive event in the entire history of Brussels. The Grand-Place was destroyed, along with 4,000 buildings—a third of all the buildings in the city. The reconstruction of  [LINK: (the city centre) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pentagon_(Brussels)] , effected during subsequent years, profoundly changed its appearance and left numerous traces still visible today. [LINK: ([70]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTECulotHennautDemanetMierop1992-73]

During the  [LINK: (War of the Spanish Succession) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_of_the_Spanish_Succession]  Brussels fell in Anglo-Dutch hands following the  [LINK: (Battle of Ramillies) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Ramillies]  in 1706. In 1708, Brussels again sustained a  [LINK: (French attack) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assault_on_Brussels_(1708)] , which it repelled. Following the  [LINK: (Treaty of Utrecht) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treaty_of_Utrecht]  in 1713, Spanish sovereignty over the Southern Netherlands was transferred to the Austrian branch of the House of Habsburg. This event started the era of the  [LINK: (Austrian Netherlands) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Austrian_Netherlands] . Brussels  [LINK: (was captured) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siege_of_Brussels]  by France in 1746, during the  [LINK: (War of the Austrian Succession) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_of_the_Austrian_Succession] , [LINK: ([71]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTETucker2009753-74]  but was handed back to Austria three years later. It remained with Austria until 1795, when the Southern Netherlands were captured and annexed by France, and the city became the  [LINK: (chef-lieu) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prefectures_in_France]  of the  [LINK: (department of the Dyle) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyle_(department)] . [LINK: ([72]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEOudiette1804XIII-75]  [LINK: ([73]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEDuvergier1835300-76]  The French rule ended in 1815, with the defeat of  [LINK: (Napoleon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Napoleon]  on the  [LINK: (battlefield of Waterloo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Waterloo] , located south of today's Brussels-Capital Region. [LINK: ([74]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGalloyHayt200686–90-77]  With the  [LINK: (Congress of Vienna) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Congress_of_Vienna] , the Southern Netherlands joined the  [LINK: (United Kingdom of the Netherlands) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Kingdom_of_the_Netherlands] , under King  [LINK: (William I of Orange) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_I_of_the_Netherlands] . The former Dyle department became the province of  [LINK: (South Brabant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Province_of_Brabant#United_Kingdom_of_the_Netherlands] , with Brussels as its capital.

In 1830, the  [LINK: (Belgian Revolution) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_Revolution]  began in Brussels, after a performance of  [LINK: (Auber's) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daniel-Francois-Esprit_Auber]  opera  [LINK: (La Muette de Portici) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Muette_de_Portici]  at the  [LINK: (Royal Theatre of La Monnaie) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Monnaie] . [LINK: ([75]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTESlatin197953–54-78]  The city became the capital and seat of government of the new nation. South Brabant was renamed simply  [LINK: (Brabant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Province_of_Brabant] , with Brussels as its administrative centre. On 21 July 1831,  [LINK: (Leopold I) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leopold_I_of_Belgium] , the first  [LINK: (King of the Belgians) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_of_the_Belgians] , ascended the throne, [LINK: ([76]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEPirenne194830-79]  undertaking the destruction of the city walls and the construction of many buildings. [LINK: ([77]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEDemey200996-80]

Following independence, Brussels underwent many more changes. It became a financial centre, thanks to the dozens of companies launched by the  [LINK: (Société Générale de Belgique) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soci%C3%A9t%C3%A9_G%C3%A9n%C3%A9rale_de_Belgique] . The  [LINK: (Industrial Revolution) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Industrial_Revolution]  and the opening of the  [LINK: (Brussels–Charleroi Canal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels%E2%80%93Charleroi_Canal]  in 1832 brought prosperity to the city through commerce and manufacturing. [LINK: ([78]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTECharruadas2005-81]  [LINK: ([79]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEDemey200996–97-82]  The  [LINK: (Free University of Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_University_of_Brussels_(1834%E2%80%931969)]  was established in 1834 and  [LINK: (Saint-Louis University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint-Louis_University,_Brussels]  in 1858. In 1835, the  [LINK: (first passenger railway) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_rail_transport_in_Belgium]  built outside England linked the municipality of  [LINK: (Molenbeek-Saint-Jean) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Molenbeek-Saint-Jean]  with  [LINK: (Mechelen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mechelen] . [LINK: ([80]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWolmar201018–20-83]

During the 19th century, the population of Brussels grew considerably; from about 80,000 to more than 625,000 people for the city and its surroundings. The Senne had become a serious  [LINK: (health hazard) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Health_hazard] , and from 1867 to 1871, under the tenure of the  [LINK: (city's then-mayor) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mayor_of_the_City_of_Brussels] ,  [LINK: (Jules Anspach) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jules_Anspach] , its entire course through the urban area was  [LINK: (completely covered over) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Covering_of_the_Senne] . [LINK: ([81]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEDemey199065-84]  This allowed  [LINK: (urban renewal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urban_renewal]  and the construction of modern buildings of  [LINK: (Haussmann) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haussmann%27s_renovation_of_Paris] -esque style along grand  [LINK: (central boulevards) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_Boulevards_of_Brussels] , characteristic of downtown Brussels today. [LINK: ([82]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEEggericx19975-85]  Buildings such as the  [LINK: (Bourse Palace) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bourse_Palace]  (1873), the  [LINK: (Palace of Justice) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palace_of_Justice,_Brussels]  (1883) and  [LINK: (Saint Mary's Royal Church) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Mary%27s_Royal_Church]  (1885) date from this period. This development continued throughout the reign of King  [LINK: (Leopold II) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leopold_II_of_Belgium] . The  [LINK: (International Exposition of 1897) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_International_Exposition_(1897)]  contributed to the promotion of the infrastructure. [LINK: ([83]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTESchroeder-GudehusRasmussen1992-86]  Among other things, the  [LINK: (Palace of the Colonies) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Africa_Palace] , today's  [LINK: (Royal Museum for Central Africa) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Museum_for_Central_Africa] , in the suburb of  [LINK: (Tervuren) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tervuren] , was connected to the capital by the construction of an  [LINK: (11 km-long (6.8 mi) grand alley) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avenue_de_Tervueren] .

Brussels was one of the major European centres of the  [LINK: (Art Nouveau) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art_Nouveau_in_Brussels]  style in the 1890s and early 1900s. [LINK: ([84]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTECulotPirlot2005-87]  The architects  [LINK: (Victor Horta) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victor_Horta] ,  [LINK: (Paul Hankar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Hankar] , and  [LINK: (Henry van de Velde) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_van_de_Velde] , among others, were known for their designs, many of which survive today. [LINK: ([85]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-:6-88]

During the 20th century, the city hosted various fairs and conferences, including the  [LINK: (Solvay Conference) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solvay_Conference]  on Physics and on Chemistry, and three  [LINK: (world's fairs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World%27s_fair] : the  [LINK: (Brussels International Exposition of 1910) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_International_Exposition_(1910)] , the  [LINK: (Brussels International Exposition of 1935) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_International_Exposition_of_1935]  and the 1958 Brussels World's Fair ( [LINK: (Expo 58) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Expo_58] ). [LINK: ([83]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTESchroeder-GudehusRasmussen1992-86]  During  [LINK: (World War I) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_War_I] , Brussels was an  [LINK: (occupied) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_occupation_of_Belgium_during_World_War_I]  city. It escaped large-scale destruction, [LINK: ([86]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-89]  although the  [LINK: (interwar period) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interwar_period]  would still see a significant amount of (re)construction. [LINK: ([d]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-90]  [LINK: ([87]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-91]  In November 1918, after the  [LINK: (German Revolution) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_revolution_of_1918%E2%80%931919]  had broken out, Brussels was embroiled in street battles between revolutionary soldiers who wanted to end the occupation and their imperialist counterparts; additionally, explosives left behind by retreating German troops damaged infrastructure and around 2,300 houses. [LINK: ([88]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-92]

During  [LINK: (World War II) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_War_II] , Brussels was  [LINK: (occupied by German forces) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/German_occupation_of_Belgium_during_World_War_II]  once again. While it was again spared major damage, a deadly bombing took place on 7 September 1943. It was carried out by the  [LINK: (American airforce) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Air_Force] , which had tried to aim at  [LINK: (SABCA) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SABCA]  facilities in  [LINK: (Haren) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haren,_Belgium] , but accidentally struck an area in  [LINK: (Ixelles) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ixelles] , killing 282 civilians. [LINK: ([89]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-93]  The city was liberated by the British  [LINK: (Guards Armoured Division) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guards_Armoured_Division]  on 3 September 1944.  [LINK: (Brussels Airport) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Airport] , in the suburb of  [LINK: (Zaventem) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zaventem] , traces its roots to  [LINK: (a German military airport) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melsbroek_Air_Base]  that was built in nearby  [LINK: (Melsbroek) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steenokkerzeel] . [LINK: ([90]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-94]

After World War II, Brussels underwent extensive modernisation. The construction of the  [LINK: (North–South connection) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North%E2%80%93South_connection] , linking the main railway stations in the city, was completed in 1952, while the first  [LINK: (premetro) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_premetro]  (underground tram) service was launched in 1969, [LINK: ([91]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-1960s-95]  and the first  [LINK: (Metro) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Metro]  line was opened in 1976. [LINK: ([92]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-1970s-96]  Starting from the early 1960s, Brussels became the de facto capital of what would become the  [LINK: (European Union) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Union]  (EU), and many modern offices were built. Development was allowed to proceed with little regard to the aesthetics of newer buildings, and numerous architectural landmarks were demolished to make way for newer buildings that often clashed with their surroundings, giving name to the process of  [LINK: (Brusselisation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brusselisation] . [LINK: ([93]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEState200451–52-97]  [LINK: ([94]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEStubbsMakaš2011121-98]

The Brussels-Capital Region was formed on 18 June 1989, after a constitutional reform in 1988. [LINK: ([95]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-99]  It is one of the three  [LINK: (federal regions of Belgium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_regions_of_Belgium] , along with  [LINK: (Flanders) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flanders]  and  [LINK: (Wallonia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wallonia] , and has bilingual status. [LINK: ([12]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-Belgian_Constitution-14]  [LINK: ([13]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-CIRB-creation-15]  The  [LINK: (yellow iris) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yellow_iris]  is the region's emblem (referring to the presence of these flowers on the city's original site) and a stylised version is featured on its official flag. [LINK: ([96]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-100]

In the 21st century, Brussels has become an important venue for international events. In 2000, it was named  [LINK: (European Capital of Culture) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Capital_of_Culture]  alongside eight other European cities. [LINK: ([97]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-101]  In 2013, the city was the site of the  [LINK: (Brussels Agreement) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Agreement_(2013)] . [LINK: ([98]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-102]  In 2014, it hosted the  [LINK: (40th G7 summit) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/40th_G7_summit] , [LINK: ([99]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-:0-103]  and in 2017, 2018 and 2021 respectively the  [LINK: (28th) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2017_Brussels_summit] ,  [LINK: (29th) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2018_Brussels_summit]  and  [LINK: (31st NATO Summits) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_Brussels_summit] . [LINK: ([100]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-104]  [LINK: ([101]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-105]  [LINK: ([102]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-:9-106]

[LINK: (On 22 March 2016) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2016_Brussels_bombings] , three coordinated  [LINK: (nail bombings) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nail_bomb]  were detonated by  [LINK: (ISIL) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISIL]  in Brussels—two at  [LINK: (Brussels Airport) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Airport]  in  [LINK: (Zaventem) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zaventem]  and one at  [LINK: (Maalbeek/Maelbeek metro station) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maalbeek/Maelbeek_metro_station] —resulting in 32 victims and three  [LINK: (suicide bombers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suicide_bombers]  killed, and 330 people were injured. It was the deadliest act of  [LINK: (terrorism) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terrorism]  in Belgium. [LINK: ([103]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-independent-107]  [LINK: ([104]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-BBC35869985-108]  [LINK: ([105]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-BBC35880119-109]  [LINK: ([106]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-Guardian220317-110]


# GEOGRAPHY #

Brussels lies in the north-central part of Belgium, about 110 km (68 mi) from the Belgian coast and about 180 km (110 mi) from the country's southern tip. It is located in the heartland of the Brabantian Plateau, about 45 km (28 mi) south of  [LINK: (Antwerp) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp]  ( [LINK: (Flanders) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flanders] ), and 50 km (31 mi) north of  [LINK: (Charleroi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charleroi]  ( [LINK: (Wallonia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wallonia] ). Its average  [LINK: (elevation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elevation]  is 57 m (187 ft) above  [LINK: (sea level) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea_level] , varying from a low point in the valley of the almost completely covered  [LINK: (Senne) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Senne_(river)] , which cuts the Brussels-Capital Region from east to west, up to high points in the  [LINK: (Sonian Forest) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonian_Forest] , on its southeastern side. In addition to the Senne, tributary streams such as the  [LINK: (Maelbeek) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maalbeek]  and the  [LINK: (Woluwe) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woluwe] , to the east of the region, account for significant elevation differences.  [LINK: (Brussels' central boulevards) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_Boulevards_of_Brussels]  are 15 m (49 ft) above sea level. [LINK: ([107]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-111]  Contrary to popular belief, the highest point (at 127.5 m (418 ft)) is not near the Place de l'Altitude Cent/Hoogte Honderdplein in  [LINK: (Forest) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forest,_Belgium] , but at the Drève des Deux Montages/Tweebergendreef in the Sonian Forest. [LINK: ([108]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-112]

Brussels experiences an  [LINK: (oceanic climate) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oceanic_climate]  ( [LINK: (Köppen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K%C3%B6ppen_climate_classification] : Cfb) with warm summers and cool winters. [LINK: ([109]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-Brussels,+Brussels+Capital,+Belgium&units=|title=Brussels,_Belgium_Climate_Classification_Köppen_(Weatherbase)-113]  Proximity to coastal areas influences the area's climate by sending marine air masses from the  [LINK: (Atlantic Ocean) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_Ocean] . Nearby wetlands also ensure a maritime temperate climate. On average (based on measurements in the period 1981–2010), there are approximately 135 days of rain per year in the Brussels-Capital Region. Snowfall is infrequent, averaging 24 days per year. The city also often experiences violent thunderstorms in the summer months.


# AS A CAPITAL #

Despite its name, the Brussels-Capital Region is not the capital of  [LINK: (Belgium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgium] . Article 194 of the  [LINK: (Belgian Constitution) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_Constitution]  establishes that the capital of Belgium is the  [LINK: (City of Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_of_Brussels] , the municipality in the region that is the city's core. [LINK: ([14]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-article_194-16]

The City of Brussels is home to many national institutions. The  [LINK: (Royal Palace of Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Palace_of_Brussels] , where the  [LINK: (King of the Belgians) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_of_the_Belgians]  exercises his prerogatives as head of state, [LINK: ([114]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-118]  is situated alongside  [LINK: (Brussels Park) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Park]  (not to be confused with the  [LINK: (Royal Palace of Laeken) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Palace_of_Laeken] , the official home of the  [LINK: (Belgian royal family) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monarchy_of_Belgium] ). [LINK: ([115]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-119]  The  [LINK: (Palace of the Nation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_Federal_Parliament]  is located on the opposite side of this park, and is the seat of the  [LINK: (Belgian Federal Parliament) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_Federal_Parliament] . [LINK: ([116]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-120]  The office of the  [LINK: (Prime Minister of Belgium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prime_Minister_of_Belgium] , colloquially called Law Street 16 (French: 16, rue de la Loi, Dutch: Wetstraat 16), is located adjacent to this building. It is also where the  [LINK: (Council of Ministers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Council_of_Ministers_(Belgium)]  holds its meetings. The  [LINK: (Court of Cassation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Court_of_Cassation_(Belgium)] , Belgium's main court, has its seat in the  [LINK: (Palace of Justice) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palace_of_Justice,_Brussels] . Other important institutions in the City of Brussels are the  [LINK: (Constitutional Court) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constitutional_Court_of_Belgium] , the  [LINK: (Council of State) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Council_of_State_(Belgium)] , the  [LINK: (Court of Audit) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Court_of_Audit_of_Belgium] , the  [LINK: (Royal Belgian Mint) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Belgian_Mint]  and the  [LINK: (National Bank of Belgium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Bank_of_Belgium] .

The City of Brussels is also the capital of both the  [LINK: (French Community of Belgium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_Community_of_Belgium]  [LINK: ([15]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-décret-17]  and the  [LINK: (Flemish Community) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flemish_Community] . [LINK: ([17]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-decreet-19]  The  [LINK: (Flemish Parliament) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flemish_Parliament]  and  [LINK: (Flemish Government) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flemish_Government]  have their seats in Brussels, [LINK: ([117]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-121]  and so do the  [LINK: (Parliament of the French Community) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parliament_of_the_French_Community]  and the  [LINK: (Government of the French Community) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Government_of_the_French_Community] .


# MUNICIPALITIES #

The 19  [LINK: (municipalities) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Municipalities_of_Belgium]  (French: communes, Dutch: gemeenten) of the Brussels-Capital  [LINK: (Region) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Communities,_regions_and_language_areas_of_Belgium#Regions]  are political subdivisions with individual responsibilities for the handling of local level duties, such as law enforcement and the upkeep of schools and roads within its borders. [LINK: ([118]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-list-122]  [LINK: ([119]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-OECD-123]  Municipal administration is also conducted by a mayor, a council, and an executive. [LINK: ([119]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-OECD-123]

In 1831, Belgium was divided into 2,739 municipalities, including the 19 currently located in the Brussels-Capital Region. [LINK: ([120]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-roots-124]  Unlike most of the municipalities in Belgium, the ones now located in the Brussels-Capital Region were not merged with others during mergers occurring in 1964, 1970, and 1975. [LINK: ([120]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-roots-124]  However, a few neighbouring municipalities have been merged into the  [LINK: (City of Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_of_Brussels] , including  [LINK: (Laeken) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laeken] ,  [LINK: (Haren) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haren,_Belgium]  and  [LINK: (Neder-Over-Heembeek) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neder-Over-Heembeek]  in 1921. [LINK: ([121]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-Brussels-125]  These comprise the northern bulge in the municipality. To the south-east is a strip of land along the  [LINK: (Avenue Louise/Louizalaan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avenue_Louise/Louizalaan]  that, in addition to the  [LINK: (Bois de la Cambre/Ter Kamerenbos) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bois_de_la_Cambre/Ter_Kamerenbos] , was annexed from  [LINK: (Ixelles) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ixelles]  in 1864. Part of the  [LINK: (Université libre de Bruxelles) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universit%C3%A9_libre_de_Bruxelles]  (ULB)'s  [LINK: (Solbosch/Solbos) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neighbourhoods_in_Brussels#Solbosch/Solbos]  campus is also part of the City of Brussels, partially accounting for the bulge in the south-eastern end.

The largest municipality in area and population is the City of Brussels, covering 33.09 km2 (12.8 sq mi) and with 198,314 inhabitants; [LINK: ([122]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-126]  the least populous is  [LINK: (Koekelberg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koekelberg]  with 22,648 inhabitants. [LINK: ([123]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-127]  The smallest in area is  [LINK: (Saint-Josse-ten-Noode) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint-Josse-ten-Noode] , which is only 1.16 km2 (0.4 sq mi), but still has the highest population density in the region, with 23,486/km2 (60,830/sq mi). [LINK: ([124]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-128]   [LINK: (Watermael-Boitsfort) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watermael-Boitsfort]  has the lowest population density in the region, with 1,953/km2 (5,060/sq mi). [LINK: ([125]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-129]

There is much controversy on the division of 19 municipalities for a highly urbanised region, which is considered as (half of) one city by most people. Some politicians mock the "19 baronies" and want to merge the municipalities under one city council and one mayor. [LINK: ([126]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-130]  [LINK: ([127]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-131]  That would lower the number of politicians needed to govern Brussels, and centralise the power over the city to make decisions easier, thus reduce the overall running costs. The current municipalities could be transformed into districts with limited responsibilities, similar to the current structure of  [LINK: (Antwerp) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp]  or to structures of other capitals like the  [LINK: (boroughs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_boroughs]  in  [LINK: (London) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London]  or  [LINK: (arrondissements) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arrondissements_of_Paris]  in  [LINK: (Paris) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris] , to keep politics close enough to the citizen. [LINK: ([128]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-132]

In the 2010s,  [LINK: (Molenbeek-Saint-Jean) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Molenbeek-Saint-Jean]  gained international attention as the  [LINK: (base) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_ISIL_terror_cell]  for  [LINK: (Islamist terrorists) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamist_terrorists]  who carried out attacks in both  [LINK: (Paris) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/November_2015_Paris_attacks]  and  [LINK: (Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2016_Brussels_bombings] . [LINK: ([129]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-133]  [LINK: ([130]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-134]  [LINK: ([131]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-135]  [LINK: ([132]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-136]  [LINK: ([133]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-137]

