# BRUSSELS-CAPITAL REGION #

The Brussels-Capital Region is one of the three federated regions of Belgium, alongside the  [LINK: (Walloon Region) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walloon_Region]  and the  [LINK: (Flemish Region) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flemish_Region] . Geographically and linguistically, it is a bilingual  [LINK: (enclave) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enclave]  in the monolingual Flemish Region. Regions are one component of Belgium's institutions; the three communities being the other component. Brussels' inhabitants deal with either the  [LINK: (French Community) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_Community_of_Belgium]  or the  [LINK: (Flemish Community) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flemish_Community]  for matters such as culture and education, as well as a  [LINK: (Common Community) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_Community_Commission]  for competencies that do not belong exclusively to either Community, such as healthcare and social  [LINK: (welfare) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Welfare_spending] .

Upon the reorganisation and split of the  [LINK: (Province of Brabant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Province_of_Brabant]  in 1995, the Brussels Region ceased to belong to any of the  [LINK: (provinces of Belgium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Provinces_of_Belgium] , nor was it subdivided into provinces itself. However, the  [LINK: (Arrondissement of Brussels-Capital) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arrondissement_of_Brussels-Capital] , whose borders coincide with the Brussels Region, continued to exist, and the areas of provincial jurisdiction were assumed by the newly created  [LINK: (Governor of the Arrondissement of Brussels-Capital) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Governor_of_the_Administrative_Arrondissement_of_Brussels-Capital]  and the Brussels regional institutions and community commissions. In 2014, the office of the Governor was formally abolished and replaced in September 2015 by the office of the "High official of the Arrondissement of Brussels-Capital", who retained responsibility for  [LINK: (crisis management) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crisis_management]  and  [LINK: (emergency planning) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emergency_planning] , while responsibility for  [LINK: (crime prevention) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crime_prevention]  was transferred to the  [LINK: (Minister-President of the Brussels-Capital Region) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minister-President_of_the_Brussels-Capital_Region] . [LINK: ([134]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-High-138]

The Brussels-Capital Region is governed by a  [LINK: (parliament) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parliament_of_the_Brussels-Capital_Region]  of 89 members (72 French-speaking, 17 Dutch-speaking—parties are organised on a linguistic basis) and an eight-member regional cabinet consisting of a  [LINK: (minister-president) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minister-President_of_the_Brussels-Capital_Region] , four ministers and three  [LINK: (state secretaries) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Secretary_of_state] . By law, the cabinet must comprise two French-speaking and two Dutch-speaking ministers, one Dutch-speaking secretary of state and two French-speaking secretaries of state. The minister-president does not count against the language quota, but in practice every minister-president has been a bilingual francophone. The regional parliament can enact  [LINK: (ordinances) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ordinance_(Belgium)]  (French: ordonnances, Dutch: ordonnanties), which have equal status as a national legislative act.

Nineteen of the 72 French-speaking members of the Brussels Parliament are also members of the  [LINK: (Parliament of the French Community of Belgium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parliament_of_the_French_Community_of_Belgium] , and, until 2004, this was also the case for six Dutch-speaking members, who were at the same time members of the  [LINK: (Flemish Parliament) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flemish_Parliament] . Now, people voting for a Flemish party have to vote separately for six directly elected members of the Flemish Parliament.

Before the creation of the Brussels-Capital Region, regional competences in the 19 municipalities were performed by the Brussels Agglomeration. The Brussels Agglomeration was an administrative division established in 1971. This decentralised administrative public body also assumed jurisdiction over areas that, elsewhere in Belgium, were exercised by municipalities or provinces. [LINK: ([135]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-139]

The Brussels Agglomeration had a separate legislative council, but the by-laws it enacted did not have legislative status. The only election of the council took place on 21 November 1971. The working of the council was subject to many difficulties caused by the linguistic and socio-economic tensions between the two communities.

After the creation of the Brussels-Capital Region, the Brussels Agglomeration was never formally abolished, although it no longer has a purpose.


# FRENCH AND FLEMISH COMMUNITIES #

The  [LINK: (French Community) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_Community_of_Belgium]  and the  [LINK: (Flemish Community) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flemish_Community]  exercise their powers in Brussels through two community-specific public authorities: the  [LINK: (French Community Commission) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_Community_Commission]  (French: Commission communautaire française or COCOF) and the  [LINK: (Flemish Community Commission) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flemish_Community_Commission]  (Dutch: Vlaamse Gemeenschapscommissie or VGC). These two bodies each have an assembly composed of the members from each linguistic group in the  [LINK: (Parliament of the Brussels-Capital Region) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parliament_of_the_Brussels-Capital_Region] . They also have a board composed of the ministers and secretaries of state from each linguistic group in the Government of the Brussels-Capital Region.

The French Community Commission also has another capacity: some of the French Community's legislative powers have been devolved to the Walloon Region (for the French language area of Belgium) and to the French Community Commission (for the bilingual language area). [LINK: ([136]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-140]  The Flemish Community, however, did the opposite; it merged the Flemish Region into the Flemish Community. [LINK: ([137]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-141]  This is related the two linguistic groups having different conceptions, with one focusing more on the Communities and the other more on the Regions, causing an asymmetrical  [LINK: (federalism) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federalism] . Because of this devolution, the French Community Commission can enact  [LINK: (decrees) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Decree] , which are legislative acts.

A bi-communitarian public authority, the  [LINK: (Common Community Commission) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_Community_Commission]  (French: Commission communautaire commune, COCOM, Dutch: Gemeenschappelijke Gemeenschapscommissie, GGC) also exists. Its assembly is composed of the members of the regional parliament, and its board are the region's ministers—not the secretaries of state—with the minister-president not having the right to vote. This commission has two capacities: it is a decentralised administrative public body, responsible for implementing cultural policies of common interest. It can give subsidies and enact  [LINK: (by-laws) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/By-law] . Additionally, it can also enact ordinances, which have equal status as a national legislative act, in the field of community welfare: in the Brussels-Capital Region, both the French Community and the Flemish Community can exercise powers in the field of welfare, but only in regard to institutions that are unilingual (for example, a private French-speaking retirement home or the Dutch-speaking hospital of the  [LINK: (Vrije Universiteit Brussel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vrije_Universiteit_Brussel] ). The Common Community Commission is responsible for policies aiming directly at private persons or at bilingual institutions (for example, the centres for social welfare of the 19 municipalities). Its ordinances have to be enacted with a majority in both linguistic groups. Failing such a majority, a new vote can be held, where a majority of at least one third in each linguistic group is sufficient.


# BRUSSELS AND THE EUROPEAN UNION #

Brussels serves as  [LINK: (de facto) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_facto]  capital of the  [LINK: (European Union) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Union]  (EU), hosting the major political  [LINK: (institutions of the Union) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Institutions_of_the_European_Union] . [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEDemey2007-1]  The EU has not declared a capital formally, though the  [LINK: (Treaty of Amsterdam) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treaty_of_Amsterdam]  formally gives Brussels the seat of the  [LINK: (European Commission) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Commission]  (the executive branch of government) and the  [LINK: (Council of the European Union) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Council_of_the_European_Union]  (a legislative institution made up from executives of member states). [LINK: ([138]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-ENA_COMM_SEAT-142] [ [LINK: (full citation needed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citing_sources#What_information_to_include] ] [LINK: ([139]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-EC_EiB-143] [ [LINK: (full citation needed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citing_sources#What_information_to_include] ] It locates the formal seat of  [LINK: (European Parliament) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Parliament]  in  [LINK: (Strasbourg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strasbourg] , where votes take place, with the council, on the proposals made by the Commission. However, meetings of political groups and committee groups are formally given to Brussels, along with a set number of plenary sessions. Three quarters of Parliament sessions now take place at its  [LINK: (Brussels hemicycle) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Espace_L%C3%A9opold] . [LINK: ([140]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-144]  Between 2002 and 2004, the  [LINK: (European Council) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Council]  also fixed its seat in the city. [LINK: ([141]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-Dragoman-145]  In 2014, the Union hosted a  [LINK: (G7 summit) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/40th_G7_summit]  in the city. [LINK: ([99]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-:0-103]

Brussels, along with  [LINK: (Luxembourg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luxembourg_City]  and Strasbourg, began to host European institutions in 1957, soon becoming the centre of activities, as the Commission and Council based their activities in what has become the  [LINK: (European Quarter) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_and_the_European_Union#European_Quarter] , in the east of the city. [LINK: ([138]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-ENA_COMM_SEAT-142]  Early building in Brussels was sporadic and uncontrolled, with little planning. The current major buildings are the  [LINK: (Berlaymont building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berlaymont_building]  of the Commission, symbolic of the quarter as a whole, the  [LINK: (Europa building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Europa_building]  of the Council and the  [LINK: (Espace Léopold) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Espace_L%C3%A9opold]  of the Parliament. [LINK: ([139]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-EC_EiB-143]  In recent years, the presence has increased considerably, with the Commission alone occupying 865,000 m2 (9,310,000 sq ft) within the European Quarter (a quarter of the total office space in Brussels). [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEDemey2007-1]  The concentration and density has caused concern that the presence of the institutions has created a  [LINK: (ghetto) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghetto]  effect in that part of the city. [LINK: ([142]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-EUO_buildings-146]  However, the European presence has contributed significantly to the importance of Brussels as an international centre. [LINK: ([143]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-E!Sarp-147]


# INTERNATIONAL INSTITUTIONS #

Brussels has, since  [LINK: (World War II) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_War_II] , become the administrative centre of many international organisations. The city is the political and administrative centre of the  [LINK: (North Atlantic Treaty Organization) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Atlantic_Treaty_Organization]  (NATO).  [LINK: (NATO's Brussels headquarters) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NATO_headquarters]  houses  [LINK: (29 embassies) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_diplomatic_missions_to_NATO]  and brings together over 4,500 staff from allied nations, their militaries, and civil service personnel. Many other international organisations such as the  [LINK: (World Customs Organization) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Customs_Organization]  and  [LINK: (Eurocontrol) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eurocontrol] , as well as international corporations, have their main institutions in the city. In addition, the main international  [LINK: (trade union) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trade_union]  confederations have their headquarters there: the  [LINK: (European Trade Union Confederation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Trade_Union_Confederation]  (ETUC), the  [LINK: (International Confederation of Free Trade Unions) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Confederation_of_Free_Trade_Unions]  (ICFTU) and the  [LINK: (World Confederation of Labour) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Confederation_of_Labour]  (WCL).

Brussels is third in the number of international conferences it hosts, [LINK: ([144]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-ULB_Int-148]  also becoming one of the largest convention centres in the world. [LINK: ([145]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-diplomatie-149]  The presence of the EU and the other international bodies has, for example, led to there being more ambassadors and journalists in Brussels than in  [LINK: (Washington, D.C.) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington,_D.C.]  [LINK: ([143]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-E!Sarp-147]  The city hosts 120 international institutions, 181  [LINK: (embassies) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embassies]  (intra muros) and more than 2,500  [LINK: (diplomats) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diplomacy] , making it the second centre of diplomatic relations in the world (after  [LINK: (New York City) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_City] ).  [LINK: (International schools) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_school]  have also been established to serve this presence. [LINK: ([145]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-diplomatie-149]  The "international community" in Brussels numbers at least 70,000 people. [LINK: ([146]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-150]  In 2009, there were an estimated 286  [LINK: (lobbying) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lobbying]  consultancies known to work in Brussels. [LINK: ([147]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-151]  Finally, Brussels has more than 1,400  [LINK: (NGOs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NGO] . [LINK: ([148]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-152]  [LINK: ([149]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-153]

The  [LINK: (Treaty of Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treaty_of_Brussels] , which was signed on 17 March 1948 between Belgium,  [LINK: (France) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/France] ,  [LINK: (Luxembourg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luxembourg] , the  [LINK: (Netherlands) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netherlands]  and the  [LINK: (United Kingdom) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Kingdom] , was a prelude to the establishment of the  [LINK: (intergovernmental) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intergovernmental_organization]   [LINK: (military alliance) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Military_alliance]  that later became the North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO). [LINK: ([150]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-154]  As of 2024 [LINK: ([update]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Brussels&action=edit] , the alliance consists of 32 independent member countries across  [LINK: (North America) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_America]  and Europe. Several countries also have diplomatic missions to NATO  [LINK: (through embassies in Belgium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_diplomatic_missions_to_NATO] . Since 1949, a number of  [LINK: (NATO Summits) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NATO_summit]  have been held in Brussels, [LINK: ([151]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-155]  the most recent taking place in June 2021. [LINK: ([102]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-:9-106]  The organisation's political and administrative  [LINK: (headquarters) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NATO_headquarters]  are located on the Boulevard Léopold III/Leopold III-laan in  [LINK: (Haren) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haren,_Belgium] , on the north-eastern perimeter of the  [LINK: (City of Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_of_Brussels] . [LINK: ([152]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-156]  A new €750 million headquarters building begun in 2010 and was completed in 2017. [LINK: ([153]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-157]

The European Organisation for the Safety of Air Navigation, commonly known as Eurocontrol, is an  [LINK: (international organisation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_organisation]  that coordinates and plans  [LINK: (air traffic control) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Air_traffic_control]  across European  [LINK: (airspace) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Airspace] . The corporation was founded in 1960 and has 41 member states. [LINK: ([154]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-History_Book-158]  Its headquarters are located in Haren, Brussels.


# DEMOGRAPHICS #

Brussels is located in one of the most  [LINK: (urbanised regions of Europe) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Banana] , between  [LINK: (Paris) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris] ,  [LINK: (London) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London] , the  [LINK: (Rhine-Ruhr) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhine-Ruhr]  (Germany), and the  [LINK: (Randstad) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Randstad]  (Netherlands). The Brussels-Capital Region has a population of around 1.2 million and has witnessed, in recent years, a remarkable increase in its population. In general, the population of Brussels is younger than the national average, and the gap between rich and poor is wider. [LINK: ([155]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-159]

Brussels is the core of a built-up area that extends well beyond the region's limits. Sometimes referred to as the urban area of Brussels (French: aire urbaine de Bruxelles, Dutch: stedelijk gebied van Brussel) or Greater Brussels (French: Grand-Bruxelles, Dutch: Groot-Brussel), this area extends over a large part of the two Brabant provinces, including much of the surrounding  [LINK: (arrondissement of Halle-Vilvoorde) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arrondissement_of_Halle-Vilvoorde]  and some small parts of the  [LINK: (arrondissement of Leuven) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arrondissement_of_Leuven]  in  [LINK: (Flemish Brabant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flemish_Brabant] , as well as the northern part of  [LINK: (Walloon Brabant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walloon_Brabant] .

The metropolitan area of Brussels is divided into three levels. Firstly, the central agglomeration (within the regional borders), with a population of 1,218,255 inhabitants. [LINK: ([27]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-:8-30]  Adding the closest suburbs (French: banlieues, Dutch: buitenwijken) gives a total population of 1,831,496. Including the outer  [LINK: (commuter) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commuter]  zone ( [LINK: (Brussels Regional Express Network) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Regional_Express_Network]  (RER/GEN) area), the population is 2,676,701. [LINK: ([29]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-BelMetr-32]  [LINK: ([30]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-:5-33]  Brussels is also part of a wider  [LINK: (diamond-shaped) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flemish_Diamond]   [LINK: (conurbation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conurbation] , with  [LINK: (Ghent) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghent] ,  [LINK: (Antwerp) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp]  and  [LINK: (Leuven) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leuven] , which has about 4.4 million inhabitants (a little more than 40% of the Belgium's total population). [LINK: ([31]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEVan_MeeterenBoussauwDerudderWitlox2016-34]  [LINK: ([156]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-Meijers-2007-160]

There have been numerous migrations towards Brussels since the end of the 18th century, when the city acted as a common destination for  [LINK: (political refugees) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_refugees]  from neighbouring or more distant countries, particularly France. [LINK: ([159]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEDumont1996307–8,_312,_319-163]  From 1871, many of the  [LINK: (Paris Communards) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Communards]  fled to Brussels, where they received political asylum. Other notable international exiles living in Brussels at the time included  [LINK: (Victor Hugo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victor_Hugo] ,  [LINK: (Karl Marx) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karl_Marx] ,  [LINK: (Pierre-Joseph Proudhon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pierre-Joseph_Proudhon] ,  [LINK: (Georges Boulanger) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georges_Boulanger] ,  [LINK: (Paul Verlaine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Verlaine] ,  [LINK: (Arthur Rimbaud) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arthur_Rimbaud] , and  [LINK: (Léon Daudet) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L%C3%A9on_Daudet] , to name a few. [LINK: ([159]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEDumont1996307–8,_312,_319-163]  [LINK: ([160]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-164]  Attracted by the industrial opportunities, many workers moved in, first from the other  [LINK: (Belgian provinces) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_provinces]  (mainly rural residents from  [LINK: (Flanders) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flanders] ) [LINK: ([161]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBuron201680–82-165]  and France, then from  [LINK: (Southern European) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southern_Europe] , and more recently from  [LINK: (Eastern European) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastern_Europe]  and  [LINK: (African) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Africa]  countries.

Since the second half of the 20th century, Brussels has been home to a large number of immigrants and  [LINK: (émigré) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89migr%C3%A9]  communities, as well as labour migrants, former foreign students or  [LINK: (expatriates) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Expatriate] , and many Belgian families in Brussels can claim at least one foreign grandparent. At the last Belgian census in 1991, 63.7% of inhabitants in Brussels-Capital Region answered that they were Belgian citizens, born as such in Belgium, indicating that more than a third of residents had not been born in the country. [LINK: ([162]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-philippevanparijs-166]  [LINK: ([163]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-ibsa-167]  According to  [LINK: (Statbel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Statbel]  (the Belgian Statistical Office), in 2020, taking into account the parents' nationality of birth, 74.3% of the population of the Brussels-Capital Region was of foreign origin and 41.8% was of non-European origin (including 28.7% of African origin). Among those aged under 18, 88% were of foreign origin and 57% of non-European origin (including 42.4% of African origin). [LINK: ([164]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-micheletribalat.fr-168]

This large concentration of immigrants and their descendants includes many of  [LINK: (Moroccan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moroccans_in_Belgium]  (mainly  [LINK: (Riffian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riffian_people]  and other  [LINK: (Berbers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berbers] ) and  [LINK: (Turkish) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turkey]  ancestry, together with French-speaking black Africans from former  [LINK: (Belgian colonies) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_overseas_colonies] , such as the  [LINK: (Democratic Republic of the Congo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democratic_Republic_of_the_Congo] ,  [LINK: (Rwanda) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rwanda]  and  [LINK: (Burundi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burundi] . Many immigrants were  [LINK: (naturalised) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naturalised]  following the great 1991 reform of the naturalisation process. In 2012, about 32% of city residents were of non-Belgian  [LINK: (European) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Europe]  origin (mainly expatriates from France, Romania, Italy, Spain, Poland, and Portugal) and 36% were of another background, mostly from Morocco, Turkey and  [LINK: (Sub-Saharan Africa) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sub-Saharan_Africa] . Among all major migrant groups from outside the EU, a majority of the permanent residents have acquired Belgian nationality. [LINK: ([165]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-169]

Brussels was historically  [LINK: (Dutch-speaking) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch-speaking] , using the  [LINK: (Brabantian dialect) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brabantian_dialect] , [LINK: ([167]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-tokyo-171]  [LINK: ([168]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-jaumain-172]  [LINK: ([169]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-roegiest-173]  but since the 19th century [LINK: ([167]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-tokyo-171]  [LINK: ([170]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-marynissen-174]   [LINK: (French) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_French]  has become the city's predominant language. [LINK: ([171]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-rudi3-175]  The main cause of this transition was the rapid  [LINK: (assimilation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cultural_assimilation]  of the local  [LINK: (Flemish population) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flemish_people] , [LINK: ([172]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-kramer-176]  [LINK: ([167]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-tokyo-171]  [LINK: ([173]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-baetens-177]  [LINK: ([174]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-ernst-178]  [LINK: ([169]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-roegiest-173]  amplified by immigration from  [LINK: (France) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/France]  and  [LINK: (Wallonia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wallonia] . [LINK: ([167]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-tokyo-171]  [LINK: ([175]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-vermeersch-179]  The  [LINK: (rise of French) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francization]  in public life gradually began by the end of the 18th century, [LINK: ([176]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-poirier-180]  [LINK: ([177]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-rousseaux-181]  quickly accelerating after  [LINK: (Belgian independence) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_independence] . [LINK: ([178]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-degroof-182]  [LINK: ([179]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-gubin-183]  Dutch — of which  [LINK: (standardisation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Standard_language]  in Belgium was still very weak [LINK: ([180]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-balans-184]  [LINK: ([181]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-busekist-185]  [LINK: ([179]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-gubin-183]  — could not compete with French, which was the exclusive language of the judiciary, the administration, the army, education, cultural life and the media, and thus necessary for  [LINK: (social mobility) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_mobility] . [LINK: ([182]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-bitsch-186]  [LINK: ([183]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-tétart-187]  [LINK: ([168]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-jaumain-172]  [LINK: ([184]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-kok-188]  [LINK: ([170]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-marynissen-174]  The value and prestige of the French language were universally acknowledged [LINK: ([168]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-jaumain-172]  [LINK: ([185]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-bogaert-189]  [LINK: ([172]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-kramer-176]  [LINK: ([179]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-gubin-183]  [LINK: ([186]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-hasquin2-190]  [LINK: ([187]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-vrints-191]  to such an extent that after 1880, [LINK: ([188]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-veron-192]  [LINK: ([189]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-velthoven-193]  [LINK: ([180]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-balans-184]  and more particularly after the turn of the 20th century, [LINK: ([179]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-gubin-183]  proficiency in French among Dutch-speakers in Brussels increased spectacularly. [LINK: ([190]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-blampain-194]

Although a majority of the population remained bilingual until the second half of the 20th century, [LINK: ([190]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-blampain-194]  [LINK: ([172]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-kramer-176]  family transmission of the historic Brabantian dialect [LINK: ([191]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-witte-195]  declined, [LINK: ([192]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTETreffers-Daller1994-196]  leading to an increase of monolingual French-speakers from 1910 onwards. [LINK: ([185]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-bogaert-189]  [LINK: ([193]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-metsenaere-thuis-197]  From the mid-20th century, the number of monolingual French-speakers surpassed the number of mostly bilingual Flemish inhabitants. [LINK: ([194]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-brio4-198]  This process of assimilation weakened after the 1960s, [LINK: ([190]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-blampain-194]  [LINK: ([195]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-mares-199]  as the  [LINK: (language border) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Language_border]  was fixed, the status of Dutch as an official language of Belgium was reinforced, [LINK: ([196]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-depré-200]  and the economic centre of gravity shifted northward to  [LINK: (Flanders) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flanders] . [LINK: ([180]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-balans-184]  [LINK: ([188]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-veron-192]  However, with the continuing arrival of immigrants and the post-war emergence of Brussels as a  [LINK: (centre of international politics) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_and_the_European_Union] , the relative position of Dutch continued to decline. [LINK: ([197]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-brusselsstudies-201]  [LINK: ([170]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-marynissen-174]  [LINK: ([198]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-janssens-202]  [LINK: ([199]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-detant-203]  [LINK: ([190]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-blampain-194]  [LINK: ([192]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTETreffers-Daller1994-196]  Furthermore, as Brussels' urban area expanded, [LINK: ([200]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-meynen-204]  a further number of Dutch-speaking municipalities in the  [LINK: (Brussels periphery) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Periphery]  also became predominantly French-speaking. [LINK: ([196]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-depré-200]  [LINK: ([201]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-klinkenberg-205]  This phenomenon of expanding Francisation — dubbed "oil slick" by its opponents [LINK: ([172]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-kramer-176]  [LINK: ([202]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-kesteloot-206]  [LINK: ([190]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-blampain-194]  — is, together with the future of Brussels, [LINK: ([203]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-frognier-207]  one of the most controversial topics in  [LINK: (Belgian politics) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_politics] . [LINK: ([188]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-veron-192]  [LINK: ([183]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-tétart-187]

Since its creation in 1989, the Brussels-Capital Region has been legally bilingual, with both French and Dutch having official status, [LINK: ([204]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-constitution-208]  as is the administration of the 19 municipalities. [LINK: ([197]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-brusselsstudies-201]  The creation of this bilingual, full-fledged region, with its own competencies and jurisdiction, had long been hampered by different visions of Belgian federalism. Nevertheless, some communitarian issues remain. [LINK: ([205]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-jacobs-209]  [LINK: ([206]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-debruycker-210]  Flemish political parties demanded, for decades, that the Flemish part of  [LINK: (Brussels-Halle-Vilvoorde) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels-Halle-Vilvoorde]  (BHV) arrondissement be separated from the Brussels Region (which made Halle-Vilvoorde a monolingual Flemish electoral and judicial district). BHV was divided mid-2012. The French-speaking population regards the language border as artificial [LINK: ([207]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-211]  and demands the extension of the bilingual region to at least all six  [LINK: (municipalities with language facilities) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Municipalities_with_language_facilities]  in the surroundings of Brussels. [LINK: ([e]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-212]  Flemish politicians have strongly rejected these proposals. [LINK: ([208]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-question-213]  [LINK: ([209]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-214]  [LINK: ([210]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-215]

Owing to migration and to its international role, Brussels is home to a large number of native speakers of languages other than French or Dutch. Currently, about half of the population speaks a home language other than these two. [LINK: ([211]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-standaard-216]  In 2013, academic research showed that approximately 17% of families spoke none of the official languages in the home, while in a further 23% a foreign language was used alongside French. The share of unilingual French-speaking families had fallen to 38% and that of Dutch-speaking families to 5%, while the percentage of bilingual Dutch-French families reached 17%. At the same time, French remains widely spoken: in 2013, French was spoken "well to perfectly" by 88% of the population, while for Dutch this percentage was only 23% (down from 33% in 2000); [LINK: ([197]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-brusselsstudies-201]  the other most commonly known languages were English (30%), Arabic (18%), Spanish (9%), German (7%) and Italian and Turkish (5% each). [LINK: ([166]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-brio2013-170]  Meanwhile, surveys from 2023 to 2024 suggest that 29% of the population speaks only languages other than French and Dutch in the home, while residents speak a total of 104 languages, up from 72 in 2001. [LINK: ([212]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-u275-217]  Despite the rise of English as a second language in Brussels, including as an unofficial compromise language between French and Dutch, as well as the working language for some of its international businesses and institutions, French remains the lingua franca and all public services are conducted exclusively in French or Dutch. [LINK: ([197]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-brusselsstudies-201]

The original dialect of Brussels (known as  [LINK: (Brusselian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brusselian_dialect] , and also sometimes referred to as Marols or Marollien), [LINK: ([51]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTETreffers-Daller199425-54]  a form of  [LINK: (Brabantic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brabantic]  (the variant of Dutch spoken in the ancient  [LINK: (Duchy of Brabant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duchy_of_Brabant] ) with a significant number of loanwords from French, still survives among a small minority of inhabitants called Brusseleers [LINK: ([52]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-evans-55]  (or Brusseleirs), many of them quite bi- and multilingual, or educated in French and not writing in Dutch. [LINK: ([213]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-winkler-218]  [LINK: ([51]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTETreffers-Daller199425-54]  The ethnic and national self-identification of Brussels' inhabitants is nonetheless sometimes quite distinct from the French and Dutch-speaking communities. For the French-speakers, it can vary from Francophone Belgian, Bruxellois [LINK: ([54]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-Lerond,_1980_pp._477-57]  (French  [LINK: (demonym) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demonym]  for an inhabitant of Brussels),  [LINK: (Walloon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walloons]  (for people who migrated from the Walloon Region at an adult age); for Flemings living in Brussels, it is mainly either Dutch-speaking Belgian, Flemish or Brusselaar (Dutch demonym for an inhabitant), and often both. For the Brusseleers, many simply consider themselves as belonging to Brussels. [LINK: ([51]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTETreffers-Daller199425-54]

[LINK: (Catholicism) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catholic_Church]  (40.0%)

[LINK: (Islam) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islam]  (23.0%)

[LINK: (Protestantism) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protestantism]  (3.00%)

Other religions (4.00%)

Non-religious (30.0%)

Historically, Brussels has been predominantly  [LINK: (Catholic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catholic_Church] , especially since the expulsion of  [LINK: (Protestants) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protestants]  in the 16th century. This is clear from the large number of historical churches in the region, particularly in the  [LINK: (City of Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_of_Brussels] . The pre-eminent Catholic  [LINK: (cathedral) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cathedral]  in Brussels is the  [LINK: (Cathedral of St. Michael and St. Gudula) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cathedral_of_St._Michael_and_St._Gudula] , serving as the  [LINK: (co-cathedral) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Co-cathedral]  of the  [LINK: (Archdiocese of Mechelen–Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archdiocese_of_Mechelen%E2%80%93Brussels] . On the north-western side of the region, the  [LINK: (National Basilica of the Sacred Heart) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basilica_of_the_Sacred_Heart,_Brussels]  is a  [LINK: (Minor Basilica) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minor_Basilica]  and  [LINK: (parish church) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parish_church] , as well as one of the  [LINK: (largest churches by area in the world) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_largest_church_buildings] . [LINK: ([215]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEStéphany200663-220]  The  [LINK: (Church of Our Lady of Laeken) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Church_of_Our_Lady_of_Laeken]  holds the tombs of many members of the  [LINK: (Belgian royal family) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monarchy_of_Belgium] , including all the former  [LINK: (Belgian monarchs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_monarchs] , within the  [LINK: (Royal Crypt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Crypt_(Belgium)] . [LINK: ([216]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEState2004218-221]  [LINK: ([217]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-222]

In reflection of its multicultural makeup, Brussels hosts a variety of religious communities, as well as large numbers of  [LINK: (atheists) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atheists]  and  [LINK: (agnostics) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agnostics] . Minority faiths include Islam,  [LINK: (Anglicanism) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anglicanism] ,  [LINK: (Eastern Orthodoxy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastern_Orthodox_Church] ,  [LINK: (Judaism) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judaism] , and Buddhism. According to a 2016 survey, approximately 40% of residents of Brussels declared themselves Catholics (12% were practising Catholics and 28% were non-practising Catholics), 30% were  [LINK: (non-religious) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Non-religious] , 23% were  [LINK: (Muslim) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muslim]  (19% practising, 4% non-practising), 3% were Protestants and 4% were of another religion. [LINK: ([214]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-2016religion-219]

As guaranteed by Belgian law, recognised religions and non-religious philosophical organisations (French: organisations laïques, Dutch: vrijzinnige levensbeschouwelijke organisaties) [LINK: ([218]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-berkley-223]  enjoy public funding and school courses. It was once the case that every pupil in an official school from 6 to 18 years old had to choose two hours per week of compulsory religious—or non-religious-inspired morals—courses. However, in 2015, the  [LINK: (Belgian Constitutional Court) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_Constitutional_Court]  ruled religious studies could no longer be required in the  [LINK: (primary) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primary_school]  and  [LINK: (secondary) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Secondary_school]  educational systems. [LINK: ([219]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-224]

Brussels has a large concentration of  [LINK: (Muslims) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islam_in_Belgium] , mostly of Moroccan, Turkish, Syrian and Guinean ancestry. The  [LINK: (Great Mosque of Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Mosque_of_Brussels] , located in the  [LINK: (Parc du Cinquantenaire/Jubelpark) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cinquantenaire] , is the oldest  [LINK: (mosque) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mosque]  in Brussels and the former seat of the Islamic and Cultural Centre of Belgium. [LINK: ([220]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-225]  Belgium does not collect statistics by ethnic background or religious beliefs, so exact figures are unknown. [LINK: ([221]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTETorrekens2007-226]  It was estimated that, in 2005, people of Muslim background living in the Brussels Region numbered 256,220 and accounted for 25.5% of the city's population, a much higher concentration than those of the other regions of Belgium. [LINK: ([222]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-BuG_100-227] [ [LINK: (better source needed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Verifiability#Questionable_sources] ]


# ARCHITECTURE #

The architecture in Brussels is diverse, and spans from the clashing combination of  [LINK: (Gothic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gothic_architecture] ,  [LINK: (Baroque) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baroque_architecture] , and  [LINK: (Louis XIV) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_XIV_style]  styles on the  [LINK: (Grand-Place) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand-Place]  to the  [LINK: (postmodern) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Postmodern_architecture]  buildings of the  [LINK: (EU institutions) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_and_the_European_Union] . [LINK: ([223]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-228]

Very little  [LINK: (medieval architecture) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medieval_architecture]  is preserved in Brussels. Buildings from that period are mostly found in the historical centre (called the Îlot Sacré),  [LINK: (Saint Géry/Sint-Goriks) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint-G%C3%A9ry_Island]  and Sainte-Catherine/Sint Katelijne neighbourhoods. The  [LINK: (Brabantine Gothic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brabantine_Gothic]   [LINK: (Cathedral of St. Michael and St. Gudula) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cathedral_of_St._Michael_and_St._Gudula]  remains a prominent feature in the skyline of downtown Brussels. Isolated portions of the  [LINK: (first city walls) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_walls_of_Brussels]  were saved from destruction and can be seen to this day. One of the only remains of the  [LINK: (second walls) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_walls_of_Brussels]  is the  [LINK: (Halle Gate) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halle_Gate] . The Grand-Place is the main attraction in the city centre and has been a  [LINK: (UNESCO World Heritage Site) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UNESCO_World_Heritage_Site]  since 1998. [LINK: ([224]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-229]  The square is dominated by the 15th century  [LINK: (Flamboyant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flamboyant]   [LINK: (Town Hall) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Town_Hall] , the  [LINK: (neo-Gothic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neo-Gothic]   [LINK: (Breadhouse) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Museum_of_the_City_of_Brussels]  and the Baroque  [LINK: (guildhalls) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guildhall]  of the former  [LINK: (Guilds of Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guilds_of_Brussels] .  [LINK: (Manneken Pis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manneken_Pis] , a fountain containing a small  [LINK: (bronze) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bronze]  sculpture of a urinating youth, is a tourist attraction and  [LINK: (symbol) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbol]  of the city. [LINK: ([225]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-230]

The  [LINK: (neoclassical) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neoclassical_architecture]  style of the 18th and 19th centuries is represented in the  [LINK: (Royal Quarter) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Quarter] / [LINK: (Coudenberg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palace_of_Coudenberg]  area, around  [LINK: (Brussels Park) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Park]  and the  [LINK: (Place Royale/Koningsplein) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Place_Royale,_Brussels] . Examples include the  [LINK: (Royal Palace) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Palace_of_Brussels] , the  [LINK: (Church of St. James on Coudenberg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Church_of_St._James_on_Coudenberg] , the  [LINK: (Palace of the Nation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palace_of_the_Nation]  (Parliament building), the  [LINK: (Academy Palace) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Academy_Palace] , the  [LINK: (Palace of Charles of Lorraine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palace_of_Charles_of_Lorraine] , the  [LINK: (Palace of the Count of Flanders) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palace_of_the_Count_of_Flanders]  and the  [LINK: (Egmont Palace) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egmont_Palace] . Other uniform neoclassical ensembles can be found around the  [LINK: (Place des Martyrs/Martelaarsplein) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Place_des_Martyrs,_Brussels]  and the  [LINK: (Place des Barricades/Barricadenplein) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Place_des_Barricades] . Some additional landmarks in the centre are the  [LINK: (Royal Saint-Hubert Galleries) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Saint-Hubert_Galleries]  (1847), one of the oldest covered shopping arcades in Europe, the  [LINK: (Congress Column) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Congress_Column]  (1859), the  [LINK: (Bourse Palace) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bourse_Palace]  (1873) and the  [LINK: (Palace of Justice) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palace_of_Justice,_Brussels]  (1883). The latter, designed by  [LINK: (Joseph Poelaert) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Poelaert] , in  [LINK: (eclectic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eclecticism_in_architecture]  style, is reputed to be the largest building constructed in the 19th century. [LINK: ([226]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-231]

Located outside the historical centre, in a greener environment bordering the  [LINK: (European Quarter) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_and_the_European_Union#European_Quarter] , are the  [LINK: (Parc du Cinquantenaire/Jubelpark) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cinquantenaire]  with its  [LINK: (memorial arcade) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arcade_du_Cinquantenaire]  and nearby museums, and in  [LINK: (Laeken) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laeken] , the  [LINK: (Royal Palace of Laeken) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Palace_of_Laeken]  and the  [LINK: (Royal Domain) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Domain_of_Laeken]  with its large  [LINK: (greenhouses) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Greenhouses_of_Laeken] , as well as the  [LINK: (Museums of the Far East) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Museums_of_the_Far_East] .

Also particularly striking are the buildings in the  [LINK: (Art Nouveau) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art_Nouveau_in_Brussels]  style, most famously by the Belgian architects  [LINK: (Victor Horta) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victor_Horta] ,  [LINK: (Paul Hankar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Hankar]  and  [LINK: (Henry Van de Velde) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Van_de_Velde] . [LINK: ([227]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-232]  [LINK: ([228]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-233]  Some of Brussels' municipalities, such as  [LINK: (Schaerbeek) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schaerbeek] ,  [LINK: (Etterbeek) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etterbeek] ,  [LINK: (Ixelles) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ixelles] , and  [LINK: (Saint-Gilles) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint-Gilles,_Belgium] , were developed during the heyday of Art Nouveau and have many buildings in that style. The  [LINK: (Major Town Houses of the Architect Victor Horta) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Major_Town_Houses_of_the_Architect_Victor_Horta] — [LINK: (Hôtel Tassel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/H%C3%B4tel_Tassel]  (1893),  [LINK: (Hôtel van Eetvelde) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/H%C3%B4tel_van_Eetvelde]  (1898),  [LINK: (Hôtel Solvay) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/H%C3%B4tel_Solvay]  (1900) and the  [LINK: (Horta Museum) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horta_Museum]  (1901)—have been listed as a  [LINK: (UNESCO) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UNESCO]   [LINK: (World Heritage Site) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Heritage_Site]  since 2000. [LINK: ([85]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-:6-88]  Another example of Brussels' Art Nouveau is the  [LINK: (Stoclet Palace) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoclet_Palace]  (1911), by the Viennese architect  [LINK: (Josef Hoffmann) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Josef_Hoffmann] , designated a World Heritage Site by UNESCO in June 2009. [LINK: ([229]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-234]

[LINK: (Art Nouveau in Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art_Nouveau_in_Brussels]

[LINK: (Hôtel Tassel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/H%C3%B4tel_Tassel]  by  [LINK: (Victor Horta) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victor_Horta]  (1893)

Stairway in the Hôtel Tassel

[LINK: (Hôtel Albert Ciamberlani) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/H%C3%B4tel_Albert_Ciamberlani]  by  [LINK: (Paul Hankar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Hankar]  (1897)

Former  [LINK: (Old England) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_England_(department_store)]  department store by  [LINK: (Paul Saintenoy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Saintenoy]  (1899)

[LINK: (Saint-Cyr House) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint-Cyr_House]  by  [LINK: (Gustave Strauven) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gustave_Strauven]  (1903)

[LINK: (Cauchie House) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cauchie_House]  by  [LINK: (Paul Cauchie) -> https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Cauchie]  (1905)

[LINK: (Sgraffito) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sgraffito]  panel in the Cauchie House

[LINK: (Stoclet Palace) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoclet_Palace]  by  [LINK: (Josef Hoffmann) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Josef_Hoffmann]  (1911)

[LINK: (Art Deco) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art_Deco_in_Brussels]  structures in Brussels include the Résidence Palace (1927) (now part of the  [LINK: (Europa building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Europa_building] ), the  [LINK: (Centre for Fine Arts) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Centre_for_Fine_Arts]  (1928), the  [LINK: (Villa Empain) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_Empain]  (1934), the  [LINK: (Town Hall of Forest) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forest_Town_Hall]  (1938), and the  [LINK: (Flagey Building) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flagey_Building]  (also known as the Radio House) on the  [LINK: (Place Eugène Flagey/Eugène Flageyplein) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Place_Eug%C3%A8ne_Flagey]  (1938) in Ixelles. Some religious buildings from the  [LINK: (interwar era) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interwar_era]  were also constructed in that style, such as the  [LINK: (Church of St. John the Baptist) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Church_of_St._John_the_Baptist,_Molenbeek]  (1932) in Molenbeek and the  [LINK: (Church of St. Augustine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Church_of_St._Augustine,_Forest]  (1935) in Forest. Completed only in 1969, and combining Art Deco with  [LINK: (neo-Byzantine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neo-Byzantine]  elements, the  [LINK: (Basilica of the Sacred Heart) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basilica_of_the_Sacred_Heart,_Brussels]  in  [LINK: (Koekelberg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koekelberg]  is one of the  [LINK: (largest churches by area in the world) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_largest_church_buildings_in_the_world] , and its  [LINK: (cupola) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cupola]  provides a panoramic view of Brussels and its outskirts. Another example are the exhibition halls of the Centenary Palace, built for the  [LINK: (1935 World's Fair) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_International_Exposition_(1935)]  on the  [LINK: (Heysel/Heizel Plateau) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heysel_Plateau]  in northern Brussels, home to the Brussels Exhibition Centre ( [LINK: (Brussels Expo) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Expo] ). [LINK: ([230]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-235]

The  [LINK: (Atomium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atomium]  is a symbolic 103 m-tall (338 ft)  [LINK: (modernist) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modern_architecture]  structure, located on the Heysel Plateau, which was originally built for the 1958 World's Fair ( [LINK: (Expo 58) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Expo_58] ). It consists of nine steel spheres connected by tubes, and forms a model of an  [LINK: (iron) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iron]   [LINK: (crystal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crystal]  (specifically, a  [LINK: (unit cell) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unit_cell] ), magnified 165 billion times. The architect  [LINK: (André Waterkeyn) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andr%C3%A9_Waterkeyn]  devoted the building to science. It is now considered a  [LINK: (landmark) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Landmark]  of Brussels. [LINK: ([231]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-236]  [LINK: ([232]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-237]  Next to the Atomium, is  [LINK: (Mini-Europe) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mini-Europe]   [LINK: (miniature park) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miniature_park] , with 1:25 scale  [LINK: (maquettes) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maquette]  of famous buildings from across Europe.

Since the second half of the 20th century, modern office towers have been built in Brussels ( [LINK: (Madou Tower) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Madou_Tower] ,  [LINK: (Rogier Tower) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rogier_Tower] ,  [LINK: (Proximus Towers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proximus_Towers] ,  [LINK: (Finance Tower) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finance_Tower] , the  [LINK: (World Trade Center) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Trade_Center_(Brussels)] , among others). There are some thirty towers, mostly concentrated in the city's  [LINK: (main business district) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_business_district] : the  [LINK: (Northern Quarter) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northern_Quarter_(Brussels)]  (also called Little Manhattan), near  [LINK: (Brussels-North railway station) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels-North_railway_station] . The  [LINK: (South Tower) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Tower_(Brussels)] , standing adjacent to  [LINK: (Brussels-South railway station) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels-South_railway_station] , is the  [LINK: (tallest building in Belgium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_tallest_structures_in_Belgium] , at 148 m (486 ft). Along the  [LINK: (North–South connection) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North%E2%80%93South_connection] , is the State Administrative Centre, an administrative complex in the  [LINK: (International Style) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Style] . The postmodern buildings of the  [LINK: (Espace Léopold) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Espace_L%C3%A9opold]  complete the picture.

The city's embrace of modern architecture translated into an ambivalent approach towards historic preservation, leading to the destruction of notable architectural landmarks, most famously the  [LINK: (Maison du Peuple/Volkshuis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maison_du_Peuple,_Brussels]  by Victor Horta, a process known as  [LINK: (Brusselisation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brusselisation] . [LINK: ([93]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEState200451–52-97]  [LINK: ([94]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEStubbsMakaš2011121-98]


# CULTURE #

Brussels  [LINK: (contains over 80 museums) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_museums_in_Brussels] . [LINK: ([233]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-238]  The  [LINK: (Royal Museums of Fine Arts) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Museums_of_Fine_Arts_of_Belgium]  has an extensive collection of various painters, such as  [LINK: (Flemish old masters) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flemish_painting]  like  [LINK: (Bruegel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pieter_Bruegel_the_Elder] ,  [LINK: (Rogier van der Weyden) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rogier_van_der_Weyden] ,  [LINK: (Robert Campin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Campin] ,  [LINK: (Anthony van Dyck) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthony_van_Dyck] ,  [LINK: (Jacob Jordaens) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacob_Jordaens] , and  [LINK: (Peter Paul Rubens) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Paul_Rubens] . The  [LINK: (Magritte Museum) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magritte_Museum]  houses the world's largest collection of works by the  [LINK: (surrealist) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surrealist]  artist  [LINK: (René Magritte) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ren%C3%A9_Magritte] . Museums dedicated to the national history of Belgium include the  [LINK: (BELvue Museum) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BELvue_Museum] , the  [LINK: (Royal Museums of Art and History) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Museums_of_Art_and_History] , and the  [LINK: (Royal Museum of the Armed Forces and Military History) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Museum_of_the_Armed_Forces_and_Military_History] . The  [LINK: (Musical Instruments Museum) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Musical_Instruments_Museum,_Brussels]  (MIM), housed in the  [LINK: (Old England) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_England_(department_store)]  building, is part of the Royal Museums of Art and History, and is internationally renowned for its collection of over 8,000 instruments.

The Brussels Museums Council is an independent body for all the museums in the Brussels-Capital Region, covering around 100 federal, private, municipal, and community museums. [LINK: ([234]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-239]  It promotes member museums through the Brussels Card (giving access to public transport and 30 of the 100 museums), the Brussels Museums Nocturnes (every Thursday from 5 p.m. to 10 p.m. from mid-September to mid-December) and the Museum Night Fever (an event for and by young people on a Saturday night in late February or early March). [LINK: ([235]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-240]

Brussels has had a distinguished artist scene for many years. The famous Belgian surrealists René Magritte and  [LINK: (Paul Delvaux) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Delvaux] , for instance, studied and lived there, as did the  [LINK: (avant-garde) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avant-garde]  dramatist  [LINK: (Michel de Ghelderode) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michel_de_Ghelderode] . The city was also home of the  [LINK: (impressionist) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Impressionist]  painter  [LINK: (Anna Boch) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anna_Boch]  from the artists' group  [LINK: (Les XX) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Les_XX] , and includes other famous Belgian painters such as  [LINK: (Léon Spilliaert) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L%C3%A9on_Spilliaert] . Brussels is also a capital of the  [LINK: (comic strip) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comic_strip] ; [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-Comic-3]  some treasured Belgian characters are  [LINK: (Tintin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Adventures_of_Tintin] ,  [LINK: (Lucky Luke) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucky_Luke] ,  [LINK: (The Smurfs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Smurfs] ,  [LINK: (Spirou) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spirou_et_Fantasio] ,  [LINK: (Gaston) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaston_(comics)] ,  [LINK: (Marsupilami) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marsupilami] ,  [LINK: (Blake and Mortimer) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blake_and_Mortimer] ,  [LINK: (Boule et Bill) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boule_et_Bill]  and  [LINK: (Cubitus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cubitus]  (see  [LINK: (Belgian comics) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_comics] ). Throughout the city, walls are painted with large motifs of comic book characters; these  [LINK: (murals) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mural]  taken together are known as  [LINK: (Brussels' Comic Book Route) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels%27_Comic_Book_Route] . [LINK: ([46]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-:1-49]  Also, the interiors of some  [LINK: (Metro stations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Metro]  are designed by artists. The  [LINK: (Belgian Comic Strip Center) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_Comic_Strip_Center]  combines two artistic leitmotifs of Brussels, being a museum devoted to Belgian comic strips, housed in the former Magasins Waucquez textile department store, designed by  [LINK: (Victor Horta) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victor_Horta]  in the  [LINK: (Art Nouveau) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art_Nouveau_in_Brussels]  style. In addition,  [LINK: (street art) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Street_art]  is changing the landscape of this multicultural city. [LINK: ([236]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-241]

Brussels is well known for its  [LINK: (performing arts) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Performing_arts]  scene, with the  [LINK: (Royal Theatre of La Monnaie) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Monnaie] , the  [LINK: (Royal Park Theatre) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Park_Theatre] , the  [LINK: (Théâtre Royal des Galeries) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Th%C3%A9%C3%A2tre_Royal_des_Galeries] , and the  [LINK: (Kaaitheater) -> https://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaaitheater]  among the most notable institutions.

The  [LINK: (Kunstenfestivaldesarts) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kunstenfestivaldesarts] , an international performing arts festival, is organised every year in May. Its main hub is the Kaaitheater, but performances and artworks are also hosted in around 30 venues throughout the city. [LINK: ([237]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-242]  [LINK: ([238]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-243]

The  [LINK: (King Baudouin Stadium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_Baudouin_Stadium]  is a concert and competition facility with a 50,000 seat capacity, the largest in Belgium. The site was formerly occupied by the  [LINK: (Heysel Stadium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heysel_Stadium] . The  [LINK: (Centre for Fine Arts) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Centre_for_Fine_Arts]  (often referred to as BOZAR in French or PSK in Dutch), a multi-purpose centre for theatre, cinema, music, literature, and art exhibitions, is home to the  [LINK: (National Orchestra of Belgium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Orchestra_of_Belgium]  and to the annual  [LINK: (Queen Elisabeth Competition) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queen_Elisabeth_Competition]  for  [LINK: (classical) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Classical_music]  singers and instrumentalists, one of the most challenging and prestigious competitions of the kind. Studio 4 in  [LINK: (Le Flagey) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Le_Flagey]  cultural centre hosts the  [LINK: (Brussels Philharmonic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Philharmonic] . [LINK: ([239]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-244]  [LINK: ([240]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-245]

Other concert venues include  [LINK: (Forest National/Vorst Nationaal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forest_National] , the  [LINK: (Ancienne Belgique) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancienne_Belgique] , the  [LINK: (Cirque Royal/Koninklijk Circus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cirque_Royal] , the  [LINK: (Botanique) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Le_Botanique]  and  [LINK: (Palais 12/Paleis 12) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palais_12] . Furthermore, the  [LINK: (Jazz Station) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jazz_Station]  in  [LINK: (Saint-Josse-ten-Noode) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint-Josse-ten-Noode]  is a museum and archive on  [LINK: (jazz) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jazz] , and a venue for jazz concerts. [LINK: ([241]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-246]

Many events are organised or hosted in Brussels throughout the year. In addition, many festivals animate the Brussels scene.

The  [LINK: (Iris Festival) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iris_Festival]  is the official festival of the Brussels-Capital Region and is held annually in spring. [LINK: ([242]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-247]  The International Fantastic Film Festival of Brussels (BIFFF) is organised during the Easter holidays [LINK: ([243]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-248]  and the  [LINK: (Magritte Awards) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magritte_Award]  in February. The  [LINK: (Festival of Europe) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Festival_of_Europe] , an open day and activities in and around the  [LINK: (European Union) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Union] 's institutions, is held on 9 May. On  [LINK: (Belgian National Day) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_National_Day] , on 21 July, a military parade and celebrations take place on the Place des Palais/Paleizenplein and in  [LINK: (Brussels Park) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Park] , ending with a fireworks display in the evening.

Some summer festivities include  [LINK: (Couleur Café Festival) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Couleur_Caf%C3%A9_Festival] , a festival of  [LINK: (world) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_music]  and  [LINK: (urban music) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urban_music] , around the end of June or early July, the  [LINK: (Brussels Summer Festival) -> https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Summer_Festival]  (BSF), a music festival in August, [LINK: ([244]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-249]  the  [LINK: (Midi Fair) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midi_Fair] , the most important yearly fair in Brussels, lasting more than a month, in July and August, [LINK: ([245]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-250]  and Brussels Beach, when the banks of the  [LINK: (canal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels%E2%80%93Charleroi_Canal]  are turned into a temporary urban beach. [LINK: ([246]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-251]  Other biennial events are the  [LINK: (Zinneke Parade) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zinneke_Parade] , a colourful, multicultural parade through the city, which has been held since 2000 in May, as well as the popular  [LINK: (Flower Carpet) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flower_Carpet_(Brussels)]  at the  [LINK: (Grand-Place) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand-Place]  in August.  [LINK: (Heritage Days) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Heritage_Days]  are organised on the third weekend of September (sometimes coinciding with the car-free day) and are a good opportunity to discover the wealth of buildings, institutions and real estate in Brussels. The "Winter Wonders" animate the heart of Brussels in December; these winter activities were launched in Brussels in 2001. [LINK: ([247]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-FOOTNOTEState2004108-252]  [LINK: ([248]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-253]

Brussels' identity owes much to its rich folklore and traditions, among the liveliest in the country. [LINK: ([249]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-254]  [LINK: ([250]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-255]

The  [LINK: (Ommegang) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ommegang_of_Brussels] , a  [LINK: (medieval pageant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medieval_pageant]  and folkloric costumed procession, commemorating the  [LINK: (Joyous Entry) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joyous_Entry]  of  [LINK: (Emperor Charles V) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emperor_Charles_V]  and his son,  [LINK: (Philip II) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_II_of_Spain] , in the city in 1549, takes place every year in July. The colourful parade includes floats, traditional processional giants, such as Saint  [LINK: (Michael) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_(archangel)]  and Saint  [LINK: (Gudula) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gudula] , and scores of folkloric groups, either on foot or on horseback, dressed in medieval garb. The parade concludes with a large spectacle at the  [LINK: (Grand-Place) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand-Place] . Since 2019, it has been recognised as a  [LINK: (Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Masterpiece_of_the_Oral_and_Intangible_Heritage_of_Humanity]  by  [LINK: (UNESCO) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UNESCO] . [LINK: ([251]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-256]

The  [LINK: (Meyboom) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meyboom] , an even-older folk tradition of Brussels (1308), celebrating the " [LINK: (May tree) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maypole] "—in fact, a corruption of the Dutch word, meaning tree of joy—takes place paradoxically on 9 August. After parading a young  [LINK: (beech) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beech]  in the city, it is planted in a joyful spirit with lots of music, Brusseleir songs, and processional giants. It has also been recognised as an expression of  [LINK: (intangible cultural heritage) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intangible_cultural_heritage]  by UNESCO, as part of the bi-national inscription " [LINK: (Processional giants and dragons in Belgium and France) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Processional_giants_and_dragons_in_Belgium_and_France] ". [LINK: ([252]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-257]  [LINK: ([253]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-258]  The celebration is reminiscent of the town's long-standing (folkloric) feud with  [LINK: (Leuven) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leuven] , which dates back to the  [LINK: (Middle Ages) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Middle_Ages] .

[LINK: (Saint Verhaegen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Verhaegen]  (often shortened to St V), a folkloric student procession, celebrating the anniversary of the founding of the  [LINK: (Université libre de Bruxelles) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universit%C3%A9_libre_de_Bruxelles]  (ULB) and the  [LINK: (Vrije Universiteit Brussel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vrije_Universiteit_Brussel]  (VUB), is held on 20 November. Since 2019, it has also been listed as intangible cultural heritage of the Brussels-Capital Region. [LINK: ([254]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-259]  [LINK: ([255]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-260]  [LINK: ([256]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-261]

Another good introduction to the Brusseleir  [LINK: (local dialect) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brusselian_dialect]  and way of life can be obtained at the  [LINK: (Royal Theatre Toone) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Theatre_Toone] , a folkloric theatre of  [LINK: (marionettes) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marionette] , located a stone's throw away from the Grand-Place. [LINK: ([257]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-262]  Finally, two famous folkloric plays,  [LINK: (Le Mariage de Mademoiselle Beulemans) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Le_Mariage_de_Mademoiselle_Beulemans]  by Frantz Fonson and Fernand Wicheler, and  [LINK: (Bossemans et Coppenolle) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bossemans_et_Coppenolle]  by Joris d'Hanswyck and Paul Van Stalle, are still the subject of regular revivals. [LINK: ([258]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-263]


# CUISINE #

Brussels is well known for its local  [LINK: (waffle) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_waffle] , its  [LINK: (chocolate) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_chocolate] , its  [LINK: (French fries) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_fries]  and its numerous types of  [LINK: (beers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beer_in_Belgium] . The  [LINK: (Brussels sprout) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_sprout] , which has long been popular in Brussels, and may have originated there, is also named after the city. [LINK: ([259]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-264]

Owing to Brussels' cosmopolitan population, almost every national cuisine in the world can be found there. The gastronomic offer includes approximately 1,800  [LINK: (restaurants) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Restaurant]  (including three 2-starred and ten 1-starred  [LINK: (Michelin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michelin_Guide]  restaurants), [LINK: ([260]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-265]  and a number of bars. In addition to the traditional restaurants, there are many  [LINK: (cafés) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caf%C3%A9s] ,  [LINK: (bistros) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bistro]  and the usual range of international  [LINK: (fast food) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fast_food_restaurant]  chains. The cafés are similar to bars, and offer beer and light dishes;  [LINK: (coffee houses) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coffee_houses]  are called salons de thé (literally "tea salons"). Also widespread are  [LINK: (brasseries) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brasserie] , which usually offer a variety of beers and typical national dishes.

[LINK: (Belgian cuisine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_cuisine]  is known among connoisseurs as one of the best in Europe. It is characterised by the combination of  [LINK: (French cuisine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_cuisine]  with the more hearty Flemish fare. Notable specialities include  [LINK: (Brussels waffles) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_waffle]  (gaufres) and  [LINK: (mussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mussels]  (usually as  [LINK: (moules-frites) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moules_frites] , served with fries). The city is a stronghold of  [LINK: (chocolate) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_chocolate]  and  [LINK: (praline) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Praline_(Belgian_chocolate)]  manufacturers with renowned companies like  [LINK: (Côte d'Or) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C%C3%B4te_d%27Or_(chocolate)] ,  [LINK: (Neuhaus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chocolatier_Neuhaus] ,  [LINK: (Leonidas) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leonidas_(chocolate_maker)]  and  [LINK: (Godiva) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Godiva_Chocolatier] . Pralines were first introduced in 1912 by Jean Neuhaus II, a Belgian  [LINK: (chocolatier) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chocolatier]  of Swiss origin, in the  [LINK: (Royal Saint-Hubert Galleries) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Saint-Hubert_Galleries] . [LINK: ([261]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-266]  Numerous  [LINK: (friteries) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friteries]  are spread throughout the city, and in tourist areas, fresh hot waffles are also sold on the street.

As well as other  [LINK: (Belgian beers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beer_in_Belgium] , the  [LINK: (spontaneously fermented) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spontaneously_fermented]   [LINK: (lambic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lambic]  style, brewed in and around Brussels, is widely available there and in the nearby  [LINK: (Senne) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Senne_(river)]  valley where the wild  [LINK: (yeasts) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yeast]  that ferment it have their origin. [LINK: ([262]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-Jackson1988-267]   [LINK: (Kriek) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kriek] , a cherry lambic, is available in almost every bar or restaurant in Brussels.

Brussels is known as the birthplace of the  [LINK: (Belgian endive) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_endive] . The technique for growing  [LINK: (blanched) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blanching_(horticulture)]  endives was accidentally discovered in the 1850s at the  [LINK: (Botanical Garden of Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Botanical_Garden_of_Brussels]  in  [LINK: (Saint-Josse-ten-Noode) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint-Josse-ten-Noode] . [LINK: ([263]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-268]


# SHOPPING #

Famous  [LINK: (shopping) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shopping]  areas in Brussels include the pedestrian-only  [LINK: (Rue Neuve/Nieuwstraat) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rue_Neuve_(Brussels)] , the second busiest shopping street in Belgium (after the  [LINK: (Meir) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meir,_Antwerp] , in  [LINK: (Antwerp) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp] ) with a weekly average of 230,000 visitors, [LINK: ([264]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-269]  [LINK: ([265]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-270]  home to popular international chains ( [LINK: (H&M) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/H%26M] ,  [LINK: (C&A) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C%26A] ,  [LINK: (Zara) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zara_(retailer)] ,  [LINK: (Primark) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primark] ), as well as the City 2 and Anspach galleries. [LINK: ([266]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-:3-271]  The  [LINK: (Royal Saint-Hubert Galleries) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Saint-Hubert_Galleries]  hold a variety of luxury shops and some six million people stroll through them each year. [LINK: ([267]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-272]  The neighbourhood around the Rue Antoine Dansaert/Antoine Dansaertstraat has become, in recent years, a focal point for fashion and design; [LINK: ([268]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-273]  this main street and its side streets also feature Belgium's young and most happening artistic talent. [LINK: ([269]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-274]

In Ixelles, the Avenue de la Toison d'Or/Gulden-Vlieslaan and the  [LINK: (Namur Gate) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Namur_Gate]  area offer a blend of luxury shops, fast food restaurants and entertainment venues, and the Chaussée d'Ixelles/Elsenesteenweg, in the mainly-Congolese  [LINK: (Matongé) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ixelles#Matongé]  district, offers a taste of  [LINK: (African fashion) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/African_fashion]  and lifestyle. The nearby  [LINK: (Avenue Louise/Louizalaan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avenue_Louise/Louizalaan]  is lined with high-end fashion stores and boutiques, making it one of the most expensive streets in Belgium. [LINK: ([270]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-275]

There are shopping centres outside the inner ring: Basilix, Woluwe Shopping Center, Westland Shopping Center, and Docks Bruxsel, which opened in October 2017. [LINK: ([266]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-:3-271]  In addition, Brussels ranks as one of Europe's best capital cities for  [LINK: (flea market) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flea_market]  shopping. The Old Market, on the  [LINK: (Place du Jeu de Balle/Vossenplein) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Place_du_Jeu_de_Balle] , in the  [LINK: (Marolles/Marollen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marollen]  neighbourhood, is particularly renowned. [LINK: ([271]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-276]  The nearby  [LINK: (Sablon/Zavel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sablon_(Brussels)]  area is home to many of Brussels'  [LINK: (antique) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antique]  dealers. [LINK: ([272]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-277]  The Midi Market around  [LINK: (Brussels-South station) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels-South_station]  and the Boulevard du Midi/Zuidlaan is reputed to be one of the largest markets in Europe. [LINK: ([273]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-278]


# SPORTS #

Sport in Brussels is under the responsibility of the  [LINK: (Communities) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Communities,_regions_and_language_areas_of_Belgium] . The Administration de l'Éducation Physique et du Sport ( [LINK: (ADEPS) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ADEPS] ) is responsible for recognising the various French-speaking sports federations and also runs three sports centres in the Brussels-Capital Region. [LINK: ([274]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-279]  Its Dutch-speaking counterpart is Sport Vlaanderen (formerly called  [LINK: (BLOSO) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bloso] ). [LINK: ([275]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-280]

The  [LINK: (King Baudouin Stadium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_Baudouin_Stadium]  (formerly the Heysel Stadium) is the largest in the country and home to the national teams in  [LINK: (football) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgium_national_football_team]  and  [LINK: (rugby union) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgium_national_rugby_union_team] . [LINK: ([276]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-281]  It hosted the final of the  [LINK: (1972 UEFA European Football Championship) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1972_UEFA_European_Football_Championship] , and the opening game of  [LINK: (the 2000 edition) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euro_2000] . Several European club finals have been held at the ground, including the  [LINK: (1985 European Cup Final) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1985_European_Cup_Final] , which saw 39 deaths due to hooliganism and structural collapse. [LINK: ([277]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-282]  The King Baudouin Stadium is also home of the annual  [LINK: (Memorial Van Damme) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memorial_Van_Damme]  athletics event, Belgium's foremost  [LINK: (track and field) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Track_and_field]  competition, which is part of the  [LINK: (Diamond League) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diamond_League] . Other important athletics events are the  [LINK: (Brussels Marathon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Marathon]  [LINK: ([278]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-283]  and the  [LINK: (20 km of Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/20_km_of_Brussels] , an annual run with 30,000 participants. [LINK: ([279]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-284]

[LINK: (R.S.C. Anderlecht) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/R.S.C._Anderlecht] , based in the  [LINK: (Constant Vanden Stock Stadium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constant_Vanden_Stock_Stadium]  in  [LINK: (Anderlecht) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anderlecht] , is the most successful Belgian football club in the  [LINK: (Belgian Pro League) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_Pro_League] , with 34 titles. [LINK: ([280]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-285]  It has also won the most major European tournaments for a Belgian side, with 6 European titles. Brussels is also home to  [LINK: (Union Saint-Gilloise) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Union_Saint-Gilloise] , the most successful Belgian club before World War II, with 11 titles. [LINK: ([281]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-286]  The club was founded in  [LINK: (Saint-Gilles) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint-Gilles,_Belgium]  but is based in nearby  [LINK: (Forest) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forest,_Belgium] , and plays in the Belgian Pro League.  [LINK: (Racing White Daring Molenbeek) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RWDM47] , based in  [LINK: (Molenbeek-Saint-Jean) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Molenbeek-Saint-Jean] , and often referred to as RWDM, is a very popular football club that, since 2023, is back playing in the Belgian Pro League. [LINK: ([282]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-287]  [LINK: ([283]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-288]

Other Brussels clubs that played in the national series over the years were  [LINK: (Royal White Star Bruxelles) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_White_Star_Bruxelles] , Ixelles SC,  [LINK: (Crossing Club de Schaerbeek) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crossing_Club_de_Schaerbeek]  (born from a merger between RCS de Schaerbeek and Crossing Club Molenbeek), Scup Jette, RUS de Laeken, Racing Jet de Bruxelles, AS Auderghem, KV Wosjot Woluwe and FC Ganshoren.

Brussels is home to notable  [LINK: (cycling races) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cycling_race] . The city is the arrival location of the  [LINK: (Brussels Cycling Classic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Cycling_Classic] , formerly known as Paris–Brussels, which is one of the oldest  [LINK: (semi classic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Classic_cycle_races]   [LINK: (bicycle races) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Road_bicycle_racing]  on the international calendar. [LINK: ([284]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-289]  From World War I until the early 1970s, the  [LINK: (Six Days of Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Six_Days_of_Brussels]  was organised regularly. In the last decades of the 20th century, the  [LINK: (Grand Prix Eddy Merckx) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Prix_Eddy_Merckx]  was also held in Brussels.


# ECONOMY #

Serving as the centre of administration for Belgium and Europe, Brussels' economy is largely  [LINK: (service) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tertiary_sector_of_the_economy] -oriented. It is dominated by regional and world headquarters of  [LINK: (multinationals) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multinational_corporation] , by European institutions, by various local and federal administrations, and by related services companies, though it does have a number of notable  [LINK: (craft) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Craft]  industries, such as the  [LINK: (Cantillon Brewery) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cantillon_Brewery] , a  [LINK: (lambic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lambic]  brewery founded in 1900. [LINK: ([285]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-290]

Brussels has a robust economy. The region contributes to one fifth of Belgium's  [LINK: (GDP) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GDP] , and its 550,000 jobs account for 17.7% of Belgium's employment. [LINK: ([286]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-291]  Its  [LINK: (GDP per capita) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GDP_per_capita]  is nearly double that of Belgium as a whole, [LINK: ([25]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-:4-28]  and it has the highest GDP per capita of any  [LINK: (NUTS 1) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nomenclature_of_Territorial_Units_for_Statistics]  region in the EU, at ~$80,000 in 2016. [LINK: ([287]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-292]  However, the GDP is boosted by a massive inflow of  [LINK: (commuters) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commuters]  from neighbouring regions; over half of those who work in Brussels live in Flanders or Wallonia, with 230,000 and 130,000 commuters per day respectively. Conversely, only 16.0% of Brussels residents work outside the region (68,827 (68.5%) of them in Flanders and 21,035 (31.5%) in Wallonia). [LINK: ([288]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-293]  Not all of the wealth generated in Brussels remains in the region, and as of December 2013 [LINK: ([update]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Brussels&action=edit] , the unemployment among its residents is 20.4%. [LINK: ([289]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-294]

There are approximately 50,000  [LINK: (businesses) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Business]  in Brussels, of which around 2,200 are foreign. This number is constantly increasing and can well explain the role of Brussels in Europe. The city's  [LINK: (infrastructure) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infrastructure]  is very favourable in terms of starting up a new business. House prices have also increased in recent years, especially with the increase of young  [LINK: (professionals) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Professional]  settling down in Brussels, making it the most expensive city to live in Belgium. [LINK: ([290]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-295]  In addition, Brussels holds more than 1,000 business conferences annually, making it the ninth most popular conference city in Europe. [LINK: ([291]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-296]

Brussels is rated as the 34th most important financial centre in the world as of 2020, according to the  [LINK: (Global Financial Centres Index) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_Financial_Centres_Index] . The  [LINK: (Brussels Stock Exchange) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Stock_Exchange] , abbreviated to BSE and now called Euronext Brussels, is part of the European stock exchange  [LINK: (Euronext) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euronext] , along with  [LINK: (Paris Bourse) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris_Bourse] ,  [LINK: (Lisbon Stock Exchange) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lisbon_Stock_Exchange]  and  [LINK: (Amsterdam Stock Exchange) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amsterdam_Stock_Exchange] . Its benchmark  [LINK: (stock market index) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stock_market_index]  is the  [LINK: (BEL20) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BEL20] .


# MEDIA #

Brussels is a centre of both media and communications in Belgium, with many Belgian television stations, radio stations,  [LINK: (newspapers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newspaper]  and telephone companies having their headquarters in the region. The French-language  [LINK: (public broadcaster) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public_broadcaster]   [LINK: (RTBF) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RTBF] , the Dutch-language public broadcaster  [LINK: (VRT) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vlaamse_Radio-_en_Televisieomroeporganisatie] , the two regional channels BX1 (formerly Télé Bruxelles) [LINK: ([292]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-297]  and  [LINK: (Bruzz) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bruzz]  (formerly TV Brussel), [LINK: ([293]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-298]  the encrypted  [LINK: (BeTV) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BeTV_(Belgium)]  channel and private channels  [LINK: (RTL-TVI) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RTL-TVI]  and  [LINK: (VTM) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/VTM_(TV_channel)]  are headquartered in Brussels. Some national newspapers such as  [LINK: (Le Soir) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Le_Soir] ,  [LINK: (La Libre) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Libre] ,  [LINK: (De Morgen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_Morgen]  and the  [LINK: (news agency) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/News_agency]   [LINK: (Belga) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belga_(news_agency)]  are based in or around Brussels. The Belgian  [LINK: (postal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mail]  company  [LINK: (bpost) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bpost] , as well as the telecommunication companies and mobile operators  [LINK: (Proximus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proximus] ,  [LINK: (Orange Belgium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_Belgium]  and  [LINK: (Telenet) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Telenet_(Belgium)]  are all located there.

As English is spoken widely, [LINK: ([42]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-BruS13EN-45]  [LINK: ([43]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-:7-46]  several English media organisations operate in Brussels. The most popular of these are the English-language daily news media platform and bi-monthly magazine  [LINK: (The Brussels Times) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Brussels_Times]  and the website  [LINK: (The Bulletin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Bulletin_(Brussels_weekly)] . Dutch-language public broadcaster VRT has also English version of its online news. The  [LINK: (multilingual) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multilingual]   [LINK: (pan-European) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pan-European_identity]  news channel  [LINK: (Euronews) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euronews]  also maintains an office in Brussels. [LINK: ([294]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-299]


# EDUCATION #

There are several  [LINK: (universities) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universities]  in Brussels. Except for the  [LINK: (Royal Military Academy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Military_Academy_(Belgium)] , a federal military college established in 1834, [LINK: ([295]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-300]  all universities in Brussels are private and autonomous. The Royal Military Academy also the only Belgian university organised on the  [LINK: (boarding school) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boarding_school]  model. [LINK: ([296]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-301]

The  [LINK: (Université libre de Bruxelles) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universit%C3%A9_libre_de_Bruxelles]  (ULB), a French-speaking university, with about 20,000 students, has three campuses in the city, [LINK: ([297]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-302]  and the  [LINK: (Vrije Universiteit Brussel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vrije_Universiteit_Brussel]  (VUB), its  [LINK: (Dutch-speaking) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch-speaking]  sister university, has about 10,000 students. [LINK: ([298]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-303]  Both universities originate from a single ancestor university, founded in 1834, namely the  [LINK: (Free University of Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_University_of_Brussels_(1834%E2%80%931969)] , which was split in 1970, at about the same time the Flemish and French Communities gained legislative power over the organisation of higher education. [LINK: ([299]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-304]

[LINK: (Saint-Louis University, Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint-Louis_University,_Brussels]  (also known as UCLouvain Saint-Louis – Bruxelles) was founded in 1858 and is specialised in social and  [LINK: (human sciences) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_science] , with 4,000 students, and located on two campuses in the  [LINK: (City of Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_of_Brussels]  and  [LINK: (Ixelles) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ixelles] . [LINK: ([300]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-305]  From September 2018 on, the university uses the name UCLouvain, together with the  [LINK: (Catholic University of Louvain) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universit%C3%A9_catholique_de_Louvain] , in the context of a merger between both universities. [LINK: ([301]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-306]

Still other universities have campuses in Brussels, such as the French-speaking Catholic University of Louvain (UCLouvain), which has 10,000 students in the city with its medical faculties at  [LINK: (UCLouvain Bruxelles Woluwe) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UCLouvain_Bruxelles_Woluwe]  since 1973, [LINK: ([302]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-307]  in addition to its  [LINK: (Faculty of Architecture, Architectural Engineering and Urban Planning) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Faculty_of_Architecture,_Architectural_Engineering_and_Urban_Planning]  [LINK: ([303]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-308]  and UCLouvain's Dutch-speaking sister  [LINK: (Katholieke Universiteit Leuven) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Katholieke_Universiteit_Leuven]  (KU Leuven) [LINK: ([304]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-309]  (offering bachelor's and master's degrees in economics & business, law, arts, and architecture; 4,400 students). In addition, the University of Kent's  [LINK: (Brussels School of International Studies) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_School_of_International_Studies]  is a specialised postgraduate school offering advanced international studies.

Also a dozen of university colleges are located in Brussels, including two drama schools, founded in 1832: the French-speaking  [LINK: (Conservatoire Royal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conservatoire_royal_de_Bruxelles]  and its  [LINK: (Dutch-speaking) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch-speaking]  equivalent, the  [LINK: (Koninklijk Conservatorium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koninklijk_Conservatorium_(Brussel)] . [LINK: ([305]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-310]  [LINK: ([306]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-311]

Most of Brussels pupils between the ages of 3 and 18 go to schools organised by the  [LINK: (French-speaking Community) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French-speaking_Community]  or the  [LINK: (Flemish Community) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flemish_Community] , with close to 80% going to French-speaking schools, and roughly 20% to Dutch-speaking schools. Due to the post-war international presence in the city, there are also a number of international schools, including the  [LINK: (International School of Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_School_of_Brussels] , with 1,450 pupils, between the ages of 2+1⁄2 and 18, [LINK: ([307]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-312]  the  [LINK: (British School of Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_School_of_Brussels] , and the four  [LINK: (European Schools) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_School] , which provide free education for the children of those working in the  [LINK: (EU institutions) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/EU_institutions] . The combined student population of the four  [LINK: (European Schools) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_School]  in Brussels is around 10,000. [LINK: ([308]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-313]

Brussels has a number of public or private-owned  [LINK: (libraries) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Libraries]  on its territory. [LINK: ([309]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-314]  Most public libraries in Brussels fall under the competence of the Communities and are usually separated between French-speaking and Dutch-speaking institutions, although some are mixed.[ [LINK: (verification needed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Verifiability] ]

The  [LINK: (Royal Library of Belgium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Library_of_Belgium]  (KBR) is the  [LINK: (national library) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_library]  of Belgium and one of the most prestigious libraries in the world. It owns several collections of historical importance, like the famous  [LINK: (Fétis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F%C3%A9tis]  archives, and is the depository for all books ever published in Belgium or abroad by Belgian authors. It is located on the  [LINK: (Mont des Arts/Kunstberg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mont_des_Arts]  in central Brussels, near the  [LINK: (Central Station) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Central_Station] . [LINK: ([310]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-315]

There are several academic libraries and archives in Brussels. The libraries of the  [LINK: (Université libre de Bruxelles) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universit%C3%A9_libre_de_Bruxelles]  (ULB) and the  [LINK: (Vrije Universiteit Brussel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vrije_Universiteit_Brussel]  (VUB) constitute the largest ensemble of university libraries in the city. In addition to the Solbosch location, there are branches in  [LINK: (La Plaine/Het Plein) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neighbourhoods_in_Brussels#La_Plaine/Het_Plein]  and  [LINK: (Erasme/Erasmus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erasmus_metro_station] . [LINK: ([311]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-316]  [LINK: ([312]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-317]  Other academic libraries include those of  [LINK: (Saint-Louis University, Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint-Louis_University,_Brussels]  [LINK: ([313]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-318]  and the  [LINK: (Catholic University of Louvain) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universit%C3%A9_catholique_de_Louvain]  (UCLouvain). [LINK: ([314]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-319]

Science and technology in Brussels is well-developed with the presence of several  [LINK: (universities) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_universities_in_Belgium]  and research institutes. The Brussels-Capital Region is home to several national science and technology institutes including the  [LINK: (National Fund for Scientific Research) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Fund_for_Scientific_Research]  (NFSR), the Institute for the Encouragement of Scientific Research and Innovation of Brussels (ISRIB), the  [LINK: (Royal Academies for Science and the Arts of Belgium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Academies_for_Science_and_the_Arts_of_Belgium]  (RASAB) and the  [LINK: (Belgian Academy Council of Applied Sciences) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_Academy_Council_of_Applied_Sciences]  (BACAS). Several science parks associated with the universities are also spread over the region.

The  [LINK: (Royal Belgian Institute of Natural Sciences) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Belgian_Institute_of_Natural_Sciences] , located in  [LINK: (Leopold Park) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leopold_Park] , houses the world's largest hall completely dedicated to  [LINK: (dinosaurs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dinosaur] , with its collection of 30  [LINK: (fossilised) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fossil]   [LINK: (Iguanodon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iguanodon]  skeletons. [LINK: ([315]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-320]  In addition, the  [LINK: (Planetarium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Planetarium_(Belgium)]  of the  [LINK: (Royal Observatory of Belgium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Observatory_of_Belgium]  (part of the institutions of the  [LINK: (Belgian Federal Science Policy Office) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgian_Federal_Science_Policy_Office] ), on the  [LINK: (Heysel Plateau) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heysel_Plateau]  in  [LINK: (Laeken) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laeken] , is one of the largest in Europe. [LINK: ([316]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-321]

Brussels is home to a thriving  [LINK: (pharmaceutical) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pharmaceutical_industry]  and  [LINK: (health care) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Health_care]  industry, which includes pioneering  [LINK: (biotechnology) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biotechnology]  research. The health sector employs 70,000 employees in 30,000 companies. There are 3,000 life sciences researchers in the city and two large  [LINK: (science parks) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Science_park] : Da Vinci Research Park and Erasmus Research Park. There are five  [LINK: (university hospitals) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_hospitals] , a  [LINK: (military hospital) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Military_hospital]  and more than 40 general hospitals and specialist clinics. [LINK: ([317]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-322]

Due to  [LINK: (its bilingual nature) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Languages_of_Belgium] , hospitals in the Brussels-Capital Region can be either monolingual French, monolingual Dutch, or bilingual, depending on their nature. University hospitals belong to one of the two  [LINK: (linguistic communities) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Communities,_regions_and_language_areas_of_Belgium]  and are thus monolingual French or Dutch by law. Other hospitals managed by a public authority must be legally bilingual. Private hospitals are legally not bound to either language, but most cater to both. However, all hospital emergency services in the Capital Region (whether part of a public or private hospital) are required to be bilingual, since patients transported by emergency  [LINK: (ambulance) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ambulance]  cannot choose the hospital they will be brought to. [LINK: ([318]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-323]


# TRANSPORT #

Brussels has an extensive network of both private or public transportation means. Public transportation includes  [LINK: (Brussels buses) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_buses] ,  [LINK: (trams) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_trams] , and  [LINK: (metro) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Metro]  (all three operated by the  [LINK: (Brussels Intercommunal Transport Company) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Intercommunal_Transport_Company]  (STIB/MIVB)), as well as a set of railway lines (operated by  [LINK: (Infrabel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infrabel] ) and railway stations served by public trains (operated by the  [LINK: (National Railway Company of Belgium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Railway_Company_of_Belgium]  (NMBS/SNCB)). Air transport is available via one of the city's two airports ( [LINK: (Brussels Airport) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Airport]  and  [LINK: (Brussels South Charleroi Airport) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_South_Charleroi_Airport] ), and boat transport is available via the  [LINK: (Port of Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_of_Brussels] . Bicycle-sharing and car-sharing public systems are also available.

The complexity of the Belgian political landscape makes some transportation issues difficult to solve. The Brussels-Capital Region is surrounded by the  [LINK: (Flemish) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flemish_Region]  and  [LINK: (Walloon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wallonia]  regions, which means that the airports, as well as many roads serving Brussels (most notably the  [LINK: (Brussels Ring) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Ring] ), are located in the other two Belgian regions. The city is relatively car-dependent by northern European standards and is considered to be the most congested city in the world according to the  [LINK: (INRIX) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/INRIX]  traffic survey. [LINK: ([319]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-auto-324]

The Brussels-Capital Region is served by two airports, both located outside of the region's administrative territory. The most notable is  [LINK: (Brussels Airport) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Airport] , located in the nearby Flemish municipality of  [LINK: (Zaventem) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zaventem] , 12 km (10 mi) east of the capital, which can be accessed by motorway (A201), train, taxi and bus. Brussels National Airport has its own railway station. This station is located in the underground (level -1) of the airport terminal building itself. The secondary airport is  [LINK: (Brussels South Charleroi Airport) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_South_Charleroi_Airport] , located in  [LINK: (Gosselies) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gosselies] , a part of the city of  [LINK: (Charleroi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charleroi]  ( [LINK: (Wallonia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wallonia] ), some 50 km (30 mi) south-west of Brussels, which can be accessed by motorway (E19 then E420) or a private bus. There is also  [LINK: (Melsbroek Air Base) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melsbroek_Air_Base] , located in  [LINK: (Steenokkerzeel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steenokkerzeel] , a military airport that shares its infrastructure with Brussels Airport. The aforementioned airports are also the main airports of Belgium. [LINK: ([320]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-325]

Since the 16th century, Brussels has had its own harbour, the  [LINK: (Port of Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_of_Brussels] . It has been enlarged throughout the centuries to become the second Belgian inland port. Historically situated near the  [LINK: (Place Sainte-Catherine/Sint-Katelijneplein) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Place_Sainte-Catherine] , it lies today to the north-west of the region, on the  [LINK: (Brussels–Scheldt Maritime Canal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels%E2%80%93Scheldt_Maritime_Canal]  (commonly called Willebroek Canal), which connects Brussels to  [LINK: (Antwerp) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp]  via the  [LINK: (Scheldt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scheldt] . Ships and large barges up to 4,500 t (9,900,000 lb) can penetrate deep into the country, avoiding break-ups and load transfers between Antwerp and the centre of Brussels, hence reducing the cost for companies using the canal, and thus offering a competitive advantage.

Moreover, the connection of the Willebroek Canal with the  [LINK: (Brussels–Charleroi Canal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels%E2%80%93Charleroi_Canal] , in the very heart of the capital, creates a north–south link, by means of waterways, between the Netherlands, Flanders and the industrial zone of  [LINK: (Hainaut) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hainaut_(province)]  ( [LINK: (Wallonia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wallonia] ). There, navigation can access the network of French canals, thanks to the important  [LINK: (inclined plane) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canal_inclined_plane]  of  [LINK: (Ronquières) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ronqui%C3%A8res_inclined_plane]  and the lifts of  [LINK: (Strépy-Bracquegnies) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Str%C3%A9py-Bracquegnies] .

The importance of river traffic in Brussels makes it possible to avoid the road equivalent of 740,000 trucks per year—almost 2,000 per day—which, in addition to easing traffic problems, represents an estimated carbon dioxide saving of 51,545 t (113,637,000 lb) per year. [LINK: ([321]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-326]

The Brussels-Capital Region has three main train stations:  [LINK: (Brussels-South) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels-South_railway_station] ,  [LINK: (Brussels-Central) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels-Central]  and  [LINK: (Brussels-North) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels-North_railway_station] , which are also the busiest of the country. [LINK: ([37]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-:2-40]  Brussels-South is also served by direct high-speed rail links: to London by  [LINK: (Eurostar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eurostar]  trains via the  [LINK: (Channel Tunnel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Channel_Tunnel] ; to  [LINK: (Amsterdam) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amsterdam]  [LINK: ([322]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-327]  by  [LINK: (Thalys) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thalys]  and InterCity connections; to Amsterdam, Paris, and  [LINK: (Cologne) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cologne]  by  [LINK: (Eurostar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eurostar] ; and to Cologne and  [LINK: (Frankfurt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frankfurt]  by the German  [LINK: (ICE) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intercity_Express] .

The train rails in Brussels go underground, near the centre, through the  [LINK: (North–South connection) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North%E2%80%93South_connection] , with Brussels Central Station also being largely underground. The tunnel itself is only six tracks wide at its narrowest point, which often causes congestion and delays due to heavy use of the route.

The  [LINK: (City of Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_of_Brussels]  has minor railway stations at  [LINK: (Bockstael) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bockstael_railway_station] ,  [LINK: (Brussels-Chapel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels-Chapel_railway_station] ,  [LINK: (Brussels-Congress) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels-Congress_railway_station] ,  [LINK: (Brussels-Luxembourg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels-Luxembourg_railway_station] ,  [LINK: (Brussels-Schuman) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schuman_station] ,  [LINK: (Brussels-West) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels-West_station] ,  [LINK: (Haren) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haren_railway_station_(Brussels)] ,  [LINK: (Haren-South) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haren-South_railway_station]  and  [LINK: (Simonis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simonis_metro_station] . In the Brussels Region, there are also railways stations at  [LINK: (Berchem-Sainte-Agathe) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berchem-Sainte-Agathe_railway_station] ,  [LINK: (Boitsfort) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boitsfort_railway_station] ,  [LINK: (Boondael) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boondael_railway_station] ,  [LINK: (Bordet (Evere)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bordet_railway_station] ,  [LINK: (Etterbeek) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etterbeek_railway_station] ,  [LINK: (Evere) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evere_railway_station] ,  [LINK: (Forest-East) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forest-East_railway_station] ,  [LINK: (Forest-South) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forest-South_railway_station] ,  [LINK: (Jette) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jette_railway_station] ,  [LINK: (Meiser (Schaerbeek)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meiser_railway_station] ,  [LINK: (Moensberg (Uccle)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moensberg_railway_station] ,  [LINK: (Saint-Job (Uccle)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint-Job_railway_station] ,  [LINK: (Schaarbeek) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schaerbeek_railway_station] ,  [LINK: (Uccle-Calevoet) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uccle-Calevoet_railway_station] ,  [LINK: (Uccle-Stalle) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uccle-Stalle_railway_station] , Vivier d'Oie-Diesdelle (Uccle),  [LINK: (Merode) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Merode_station]  and  [LINK: (Watermael) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watermael_railway_station] .

The  [LINK: (Brussels Intercommunal Transport Company) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Intercommunal_Transport_Company]  (STIB/MIVB) is the local  [LINK: (public transport) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public_transport]  operator in Brussels. It covers the 19 municipalities of the Brussels-Capital Region and some surface routes extend to the near suburbs in the other two regions, linking with the  [LINK: (De Lijn) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_Lijn]  network in  [LINK: (Flanders) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flanders]  and the  [LINK: (TEC) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TEC_(transport)]  network in  [LINK: (Wallonia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wallonia] .

The  [LINK: (Brussels Metro) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Metro]  dates back to 1976, [LINK: ([323]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-328]  but underground lines known as the  [LINK: (premetro) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_premetro]  have been serviced by tramways since 1968. It is the only  [LINK: (rapid transit) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rapid_transit]  system in Belgium ( [LINK: (Antwerp) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antwerp_Pre-metro]  and  [LINK: (Charleroi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M%C3%A9tro_L%C3%A9ger_de_Charleroi]  both having  [LINK: (light rail) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light_rail]  systems). The network consists of four conventional metro lines and three premetro lines. The metro-grade lines are  [LINK: (M1) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Metro_line_1] ,  [LINK: (M2) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Metro_line_2] ,  [LINK: (M5) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Metro_line_5] , and  [LINK: (M6) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Metro_line_6] , with some shared sections, covering a total of 40 km (25 mi). [LINK: ([324]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-The_network_and_vehicles-329]  As of 2017 [LINK: ([update]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Brussels&action=edit] , the Metro network within the region has a total of  [LINK: (69 metro and premetro stations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Brussels_Metro_stations] . The Metro is an important  [LINK: (means of transport) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transport_in_Brussels] , connecting with six  [LINK: (railway stations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Railway_station]  of the  [LINK: (National Railway Company of Belgium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Railway_Company_of_Belgium]  (NMBS/SNCB), and many tram and bus stops operated by STIB/MIVB, as well as with  [LINK: (Flemish) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flanders]   [LINK: (De Lijn) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_Lijn]  and  [LINK: (Walloon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wallonia]   [LINK: (TEC) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TEC_(transport)]  bus stops.

A comprehensive  [LINK: (bus) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_buses]  and  [LINK: (tram network) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_trams]  covers the region. As of 2017 [LINK: ([update]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Brussels&action=edit] , the Brussels tram system consists of 17 tram lines (three of which – lines  [LINK: (T4) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_tram_route_4] ,  [LINK: (T7) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_tram_route_7]  and T10 – qualify as premetro lines that partly travel over underground sections that were intended to be eventually converted into metro lines). [LINK: ([325]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-newscientist-330]  The total route length is 139 km (86 mi), [LINK: ([324]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-The_network_and_vehicles-329]  making it one of the largest tram networks in Europe. The Brussels bus network is complementary to the rail network. It consists of 50 bus routes and 11 night routes, spanning 445 km (277 mi). [LINK: ([324]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-The_network_and_vehicles-329]

Since April 2007, STIB/MIVB has also been operating a night bus network called Noctis on Friday and Saturday nights from midnight until 3 a.m. [LINK: ([326]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-noctisbrus-331]  The service consists of 11 routes (N04, N05, N06, N08, N09, N10, N11, N12, N13, N16 and N18). [LINK: ([327]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-332]  The fare on these night buses is the same as during the day. All the lines leave from the  [LINK: (Place de la Bourse/Beursplein) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Place_de_la_Bourse,_Brussels]  in the city centre at 30 minutes intervals and cover all the main streets in the capital, as they radiate outwards to the suburbs. [LINK: ([328]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-333]  Noctis services returned from 2 July 2021 after over a year of disruption due to the  [LINK: (COVID-19 pandemic in Belgium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19_pandemic_in_Belgium] . [LINK: ([326]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-noctisbrus-331]

MoBIB is the STIB/MIVB electronic smart card, introduced in 2007, replacing the discontinued paper tickets. The hourly travel fare includes all means of transport (metro, tram and bus) operated by STIB/MIVB. Each trip has a different cost depending on the type of support purchased. Passengers can purchase monthly passes, yearly passes, 1 and 10-trip tickets and daily and 3-day passes. These can be bought over the Internet, but require customers to have a smart card reader. GO vending machines accept coins, local and international chip and PIN credit and debit cards.

Moreover, a complimentary interticketing system means that a combined STIB/MIVB ticket holder can, depending on the option, also use the train network operated by NMBS/SNCB and/or long-distance buses and commuter services operated by De Lijn or TEC. With this ticket, a single journey can include multiple stages across the different modes of transport and networks.

Since 2003, Brussels has had a car-sharing service operated by the  [LINK: (Bremen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bremen]  company Cambio, in partnership with STIB/MIVB and the local ridesharing company Taxi Stop. [LINK: ([329]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-334]  In 2006, a  [LINK: (public bicycle-sharing programme) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Community_bicycle_program]  was introduced. The scheme was subsequently taken over by  [LINK: (Villo!) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villo!] . Since 2008, this night-time public transport service has been supplemented by Collecto, a shared taxi system, which operates on weekdays between 11 p.m. and 6 a.m. [LINK: ([330]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-335]  In 2012, the Zen Car electric car-sharing scheme was launched in the university and European areas, though it ceased operating in the city in 2020. [LINK: ([331]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-336]

In medieval times, Brussels stood at the intersection of routes running north–south (the modern Rue Haute/Hoogstraat) and east–west (Chaussée de Gand/Gentsesteenweg–Rue du Marché aux Herbes/Grasmarkt–Rue de Namur/Naamsestraat). The ancient pattern of streets, radiating from the  [LINK: (Grand-Place) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand-Place] , in large part remains, but has been overlaid by boulevards built  [LINK: (over the river Senne) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Covering_of_the_Senne] ,  [LINK: (over the city walls) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortifications_of_Brussels#Construction_of_the_Small_Ring]  and over the railway connection between the North and South Stations. In 2012, Brussels had the most congested traffic in Europe and North America, according to US traffic information platform  [LINK: (INRIX) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/INRIX] . [LINK: ([332]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-337]

Brussels is the hub of a range of national roads, the main ones being clockwise: the  [LINK: (N1) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/N1_road_(Belgium)]  (N to  [LINK: (Breda) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breda] ),  [LINK: (N2) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/N2_road_(Belgium)]  (E to  [LINK: (Maastricht) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maastricht] ),  [LINK: (N3) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/N3_road_(Belgium)]  (E to  [LINK: (Aachen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aachen] ),  [LINK: (N4) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/N4_road_(Belgium)]  (SE to  [LINK: (Luxembourg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luxembourg_City] ),  [LINK: (N5) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/N5_road_(Belgium)]  (S to  [LINK: (Reims) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reims] ), N6 (S to  [LINK: (Maubeuge) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maubeuge] ), N7 (SW to  [LINK: (Lille) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lille] ),  [LINK: (N8) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/N8_road_(Belgium)]  (W to  [LINK: (Koksijde) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koksijde] ) and N9 (NW to  [LINK: (Ostend) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ostend] ). [LINK: ([333]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-338]  Usually named chaussées/steenwegen, these roads normally run in a straight line, but sometimes lose themselves in a maze of narrow shopping streets. The region is skirted by the  [LINK: (European route E19) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_route_E19]  (N-S) and the  [LINK: (E40) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_route_E40]  (E-W), while the  [LINK: (E411) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/E411]  leads away to the SE. Brussels has an  [LINK: (orbital motorway) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orbital_motorway] , numbered R0 (R-zero) and commonly referred to as the  [LINK: (Ring) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Ring] . It is pear-shaped, as the southern side was never built as originally conceived, owing to residents' objections.

The city centre, sometimes known as the  [LINK: (Pentagon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pentagon_(Brussels)] , is surrounded by an inner ring road, the  [LINK: (Small Ring) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Small_Ring,_Brussels]  (French: Petite Ceinture, Dutch: Kleine Ring), a sequence of boulevards formally numbered R20. These were built upon the site of the  [LINK: (second set of city walls) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_walls_of_Brussels]  following their demolition. The  [LINK: (Metro line 2) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Metro_line_2]  runs alongside (not actually under) much of these. Since June 2015, a number of  [LINK: (central boulevards) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_Boulevards_of_Brussels]  inside the Pentagon have become car-free, limiting transit traffic through the old city. [LINK: ([334]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-339]

On the eastern side of the region, the R21 or  [LINK: (Greater Ring) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greater_ring_(Brussels)]  (French: Grande Ceinture, Dutch: Grote Ring) is formed by a string of boulevards that curves round from  [LINK: (Laeken) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laeken]  to  [LINK: (Uccle) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uccle] . Some premetro stations (see  [LINK: (Brussels Metro) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels_Metro] ) were built on that route. A little further out, a stretch numbered R22 leads from Zaventem to  [LINK: (Saint-Job) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uccle] .


# SECURITY AND EMERGENCY SERVICES #

The Brussels local police, supported by the federal police, is responsible for law enforcement in Brussels. The 19 municipalities of the Brussels-Capital Region are divided into six police zones, [LINK: ([335]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-340]  all bilingual (French and Dutch):

5339 Brussels Capital Ixelles: the  [LINK: (City of Brussels) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/City_of_Brussels]  and  [LINK: (Ixelles) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ixelles]

5340 Brussels West:  [LINK: (Berchem-Sainte-Agathe) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berchem-Sainte-Agathe] ,  [LINK: (Ganshoren) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ganshoren] ,  [LINK: (Jette) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jette] ,  [LINK: (Koekelberg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koekelberg]  and  [LINK: (Molenbeek-Saint-Jean) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Molenbeek-Saint-Jean]

5341 South:  [LINK: (Anderlecht) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anderlecht] ,  [LINK: (Forest) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forest,_Belgium]  and  [LINK: (Saint-Gilles) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint-Gilles,_Belgium]

5342 Uccle/Watermael-Boitsfort/Auderghem:  [LINK: (Auderghem) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Auderghem] ,  [LINK: (Uccle) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uccle]  and  [LINK: (Watermael-Boitsfort) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watermael-Boitsfort]

5343 Montgomery:  [LINK: (Etterbeek) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etterbeek] ,  [LINK: (Woluwe-Saint-Lambert) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woluwe-Saint-Lambert]  and  [LINK: (Woluwe-Saint-Pierre) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woluwe-Saint-Pierre]

5344 Polbruno:  [LINK: (Evere) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evere] ,  [LINK: (Saint-Josse-ten-Noode) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint-Josse-ten-Noode]  and  [LINK: (Schaerbeek) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schaerbeek]

The Brussels Fire and Emergency Medical Care Service, commonly known by its acronym SIAMU (DBDMH), operates in the 19 municipalities of Brussels. [LINK: ([336]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-341]  It is a class X fire department and the largest  [LINK: (fire service) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fire_service]  in Belgium in terms of annual operations, equipment, and personnel. It has 9  [LINK: (fire stations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fire_station] , spread over the entire Brussels-Capital Region, and employs about 1,000 professional  [LINK: (firefighters) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firefighter] . As well as preventing and fighting fires, SIAMU also provides emergency medical care services in Brussels via its centralised 100 number (and the single 112 emergency number for the 27 countries of the European Union). It is bilingual (French–Dutch).


# PARKS AND GREEN SPACES #

Brussels is one of the greenest capitals in Europe, with over 8,000 hectares of green spaces. [LINK: ([337]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-342]  Vegetation cover and natural areas are higher in the outskirts, where they have limited the  [LINK: (peri-urbanisation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peri-urbanisation]  of the capital, but they decrease sharply towards the centre of Brussels; 10% in the central  [LINK: (Pentagon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pentagon_(Brussels)] , 30% of the municipalities in the first ring, and 71% of the municipalities in the second ring are occupied by green spaces.

Many parks and gardens, both public and privately owned, are scattered throughout the city. In addition to this, the  [LINK: (Sonian Forest) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonian_Forest]  is located in its southern part and stretches out over the three  [LINK: (Belgian regions) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Communities,_regions_and_language_areas_of_Belgium#Regions] . As of 2017 [LINK: ([update]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Brussels&action=edit] , it has been inscribed as a  [LINK: (UNESCO) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UNESCO]   [LINK: (World Heritage Site) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Heritage_Site] , the only Belgian component to the multinational inscription ' [LINK: (Primeval Beech Forests of the Carpathians and Other Regions of Europe) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primeval_Beech_Forests_of_the_Carpathians_and_Other_Regions_of_Europe] '. [LINK: ([338]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brussels#cite_note-343]

